window.RSI_SEARCH = [{"lang": "en", "slug": "internal-rewards", "title": "Why Internal Rewards Can Differ from the Final Objective", "category": "Value and evaluation", "text": "Why Internal Rewards Can Differ from the Final Objective If obtaining food first requires learning to open boxes, should training reward only eating, or also opening boxes? Singh and colleagues show why a signal that helps a bounded learner acquire a skill can differ from the criterion used to evaluate it.  The objective served by a training reward One grid-world experiment initially provides no food. A learner trained only on food rewards receives little guidance; an internal reward for opening boxes can develop behavior useful when food later becomes available. Internal reward guides learning along the way, while external fitness evaluates returns over the learner’s lifetime. Let r denote an internal reward, A a specified learning algorithm, and A(r) the resulting policy. If J evaluates lifetime performance, reward design seeks an r that improves J(A(r)). The same reward can work differently for learners with different capabilities, memories, or learning speeds. Its value depends on how it helps the particular learner.  Comparing reward designs The authors search a specified reward space and compare the selected internal rewards with training directly on external fitness. Learning to open boxes in advance helps subsequent food collection: behavior without an immediate external return can improve lifetime returns. A second set of partially observable foraging experiments examines rewards that depend on the learner’s internal state. The learner and reward-search procedure remain fixed. The experiments compare how different training signals help achieve the external objective.  From internal rewards to automated reward design Eureka adopts this reward-design formulation: a language model writes rewards, policies are trained, and feedback informs revised rewards. Both approaches retain a fixed external criterion. Utility self-modification asks a further question: if that criterion can also change, which objective should determine whether a modification is better?  Source and reading guide Singh, Lewis, Barto, and Sorg (2010),  Intrinsically Motivated Reinforcement Learning: An Evolutionary Perspective . See the journal version, §IV, Equation (1), §V, and the experimental figures and tables. Journal record · Author PDF", "paper": "Singh et al. (2010). Intrinsically Motivated Reinforcement Learning: An Evolutionary Perspective. IEEE TAMD."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "eureka", "title": "Eureka: Improving Reward Programs through Training Feedback", "category": "Value and evaluation", "text": "Eureka: Improving Reward Programs through Training Feedback Giving a robot a task objective still leaves the problem of designing rewards that help it learn. Eureka uses a language model to revise reward code, guided by the results of policy training.  Changing the training signal while keeping evaluation fixed A reward program guides policy training; an external task score evaluates the trained policy. Policies produced by different rewards are compared under this fixed criterion. The objective is to improve F(A(R)), where R is a reward program, A(R) the policy trained with it, and F the task evaluation. This follows the distinction between internal rewards and external objectives.  How feedback informs the next revision The language model reads the task description and environment code, then generates candidate reward programs. A reinforcement learning algorithm trains a policy with each reward, recording task performance and training statistics for individual reward components. The language model reads this feedback alongside the reward code. Task performance indicates which designs are worth pursuing; the magnitudes and trends of reward components help connect training behavior to the code and guide subsequent revisions.  What the two comparisons establish Simply generating more candidates could also yield better rewards. The authors therefore compare one-shot generation with generation over successive rounds, using the same total number of candidates. The latter uses feedback from earlier training and performs better. This supports the contribution of feedback-driven iteration beyond the number of attempts; matching candidate counts does not match all computational costs. A second comparison retains task scores but removes detailed feedback on reward components. Performance declines, indicating that these statistics provide additional help beyond task scores in the tested tasks and search procedure. The loop revises reward code while keeping the base language model, reinforcement learning algorithm, task evaluation, and search framework fixed. Eureka connects training feedback to better training signals. Utility self-modification instead examines how to evaluate changes to the objective itself.  Source and reading guide Ma et al. (2024),  Eureka: Human-Level Reward Design via Coding Large Language Models , ICLR; arXiv v2. See Definition 2.1, §3.2–3.3, §4.1–4.3, and the feedback ablations. Paper · Full text", "paper": "Ma et al. (2024). Eureka: Human-Level Reward Design via Coding Large Language Models. ICLR; arXiv v2."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "utility-self-modification", "title": "Which Objective Should Evaluate a Change of Objective?", "category": "Value and evaluation", "text": "Which Objective Should Evaluate a Change of Objective? A system could replace “success means completing the task” with “every outcome counts as success.” Its self-evaluation would improve without any improvement in behavior. Everitt and colleagues ask which objective should evaluate a modification when an agent can change its own goals.  Three evaluation rules The first rule evaluates outcomes using the modified utility function. Under this rule, adopting a utility function that assigns maximal utility to every outcome can itself be an optimal modification. The second keeps the current utility function but predicts future behavior as though the modification had not occurred. It retains the original criterion while ignoring how the change affects subsequent actions. The third uses the current utility function and predicts the behavior that the modification would actually produce. The paper calls this  realistic  evaluation: what would the modified agent do, and would those actions be better under its present objective?  Conditions for preserving optimality under the original objective For the third rule, the paper proves that behavior along the realized history can remain optimal for the original objective, provided the initial policy is optimal and the beliefs and initial utility satisfy modification independence and the other stated conditions. Modification independence means that, with external actions and observations held fixed, utility and beliefs about the environment cannot change merely because the record of internal program modifications differs. Modifications can still affect outcomes through future actions. The theorem preserves behavioral optimality under the original objective; it does not require the utility code to remain textually unchanged.  How this differs from rewriting training rewards Eureka changes training rewards while evaluating policies against a fixed task criterion. It compares different means of pursuing the same objective. Utility self-modification allows that criterion to change as well. Evaluation rules and predictions of future behavior must therefore be specified together to distinguish better behavior from a change in how behavior is scored.  Source and reading guide Everitt, Filan, Daswani, and Hutter (2016),  Self-Modification of Policy and Utility Function in Rational Agents , AGI; arXiv v1. See §3, Assumption 8, Definitions 9–12, and Theorems 14–16. Paper · Full text", "paper": "Everitt et al. (2016). Self-Modification of Policy and Utility Function in Rational Agents. AGI; v1."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "metareasoning", "title": "Metareasoning: When Is More Computation Worthwhile?", "category": "Value and evaluation", "text": "Metareasoning: When Is More Computation Worthwhile? A game-playing program has found a promising move. Should it simulate other moves before acting? Hay and colleagues formulate this as a decision problem: computation is valuable insofar as it improves the eventual action, after accounting for its cost.  Treating computation as a decision A simulation does not place a stone on the actual board. It supplies a result that updates the program’s assessment of candidate moves. The paper treats completed computations and their results as the metalevel state. The agent can choose another computation or stop and execute its current best action. The objective is the utility of the final action minus computational costs. If a simulation is unlikely to change the choice, reducing uncertainty may offer little task value. If two promising actions remain difficult to distinguish, a computation that could change the choice is more valuable. Each computation also affects which later computations are worthwhile, so an optimal policy generally evaluates sequences of computations. A metalevel Markov decision process expresses this choice.  Allocating computation in selection problems and Go Solving the full metalevel problem is itself expensive. The authors develop approximations and heuristics, comparing allocations of simulation resources in selection problems and Go. Go offers a clear feedback setting: simulations follow known rules, and their outcomes help compare candidate moves. The problem is how to make better choices with a limited number of simulations. The cost here is internal computation, distinct from the return forgone by actually trying an external action. Search and verification can therefore improve a decision before the external action is taken.  From choosing computations to modifying programs The Gödel machine extends the choice to whether a program should modify itself now or continue searching. It also allows changes to the program that searches for proofs. Both approaches evaluate whether further computation is worthwhile; the latter can additionally change how future computation is performed.  Source and reading guide Hay et al. (2012),  Selecting Computations: Theory and Applications , UAI. See the definitions in §2 and the analyses and applications in §4–6. Publication · Full text consulted", "paper": "Hay et al. (2012). Selecting Computations: Theory and Applications. UAI."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "godel-machine", "title": "The Gödel Machine’s Criterion for Self-Modification", "category": "Value and evaluation", "text": "The Gödel Machine’s Criterion for Self-Modification After finding a potentially useful modification, should a program apply it immediately or keep searching for a better one? The Gödel machine uses a proof condition to decide when to stop searching and modify itself.  The acceptance condition The system uses axioms to describe its own operation, its environment, and its utility objective. A proof-search program seeks candidate modifications and attempts to prove that executing one now has greater expected future utility than continuing the current search process. Once such a proof is found, the system executes the modification. Replaceable components include the programs that search for modifications and proofs. A modification can therefore change how subsequent modifications are proposed, checked, and implemented.  Why the alternative is continued search Continuing to search includes the possibility of finding other proposals later. Comparing a candidate only with “never change the current program” would not explain why the system should stop searching now. The acceptance condition accounts for waiting, search, and future self-modifications, combining the stopping decision and the modification decision in one utility comparison. The optimality result is relative to the system’s axioms. A useful modification may be unprovable within them, and an existing proof may not be found in time. The result therefore governs the choice to modify once the required proof has been obtained.  Acceptance by proof and evaluation by experiment Metareasoning also connects computational costs to eventual choices; the Gödel machine additionally allows changes to the computing program itself. The modern reward-design system Eureka instead trains policies with candidate rewards and uses task performance to guide further revisions. The former accepts modifications through proof, while the latter evaluates candidates through training experiments. They provide, respectively, a formal acceptance criterion and an executable feedback process.  Source and reading guide Schmidhuber,  Gödel Machines: Self-Referential Universal Problem Solvers Making Provably Optimal Self-Improvements . First draft 2003; this account uses v5 from 2006. See §2.4, Theorem 4.1, and §4.3. Paper · Full text", "paper": "Schmidhuber (2003/2006). Gödel Machines: Self-Referential Universal Problem Solvers Making Provably Optimal Self-Improvements. v5."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "blackwell-information", "title": "Blackwell on how information improves decisions", "category": "Knowledge and interaction", "text": "Blackwell on how information improves decisions Suppose you use temperature to decide whether to switch on a machine. One sensor reports the exact temperature; another reports only whether it exceeds a threshold. A decision based on the threshold can also be made using the exact reading: convert the number into a threshold result, then apply the same decision rule. This illustrates the starting point of Blackwell's comparison: can one signal reproduce the decisions supported by another?  Simulating a coarser signal Let w be the unknown state. Channel A produces signal x, and channel B produces signal z. Suppose a fixed processing rule K can turn x into a signal with the same distribution as B, by discarding detail or adding randomness:   The two Q terms describe each channel's signal distribution when the state is w. K(z x) is the probability of producing z after receiving x. Crucially, K can use only the signal, not the unknown state w; otherwise, the processing step could introduce additional information. Take any decision rule that uses B. A decision maker with access to A can first generate z using K, then apply that rule, reproducing its performance. A therefore preserves every decision rule available under B. With the same prior and payoff function, no acquisition cost, and optimal use of information, A cannot yield a lower optimal expected payoff.  When additional information changes the outcome The conclusion is a weak improvement, not necessarily a strict one. If switching on the machine requires only a threshold judgment, the exact temperature may add no value. Extra detail can improve the outcome only if it changes which action is worth taking. The decision value of information therefore depends on the task. This argument allows the decision maker to simulate the old channel freely and choose the best rule. Learning also involves acquisition costs and limits on using information. Usable information studies how much evidence a restricted predictor can exploit; dual control studies how actions determine the evidence available later.  Source and verification scope David Blackwell (1953),  Equivalent Comparisons of Experiments , Annals of Mathematical Statistics 24: 265–272. Published paper; scholarly profile and abstract. Verification was limited to the bibliographic record and abstract. This note explains only the direct implication from signal processing to nondecreasing decision value; the paper's full equivalence theorem was not verified.", "paper": "Blackwell (1953). Equivalent Comparisons of Experiments. Ann. Math. Stat. 24:265–272. †"}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "dual-control", "title": "Dual control and the learning value of actions", "category": "Knowledge and interaction", "text": "Dual control and the learning value of actions Consider pushing an object with unknown resistance. The force affects how far it moves, while the observed displacement helps reveal its resistance. One action changes both the physical state and what the controller knows. Dual control asks whether the cheapest action now might sacrifice an experiment that would be useful later.  Why substituting an estimate is not enough Klenske and Hennig study control over a finite horizon with unknown dynamics. The decision maker tracks the current physical state and a probability distribution over unknown parameters. An action changes the state; the resulting observation updates the parameter distribution. Both changes matter for the next decision. A common simplification estimates the parameters and then controls the system as if those estimates were correct. This is certainty-equivalent control. It uses existing knowledge but does not account for how the current action might help future learning. Dual control includes that benefit: a slightly more costly action can be worthwhile if it resolves an important uncertainty and improves later actions. Planning is difficult because the next observation is unknown. Each action can produce several observations, each leading to different beliefs and subsequent decisions. The paper makes this calculation tractable through a local second-order approximation of future costs around a precomputed reference trajectory.  Testing when exploration is useful The authors compare four controllers: one given the true dynamics, a certainty-equivalent controller, a controller with an exploration bonus, and an approximate dual controller. The simulations vary the timing and cost of exploration, the relevance of unknown parameters to later tasks, and how quickly knowledge becomes outdated. Exploration can be delayed when it becomes cheaper later and the cost of waiting without that knowledge is small. A parameter that does not affect later actions warrants little exploration cost. Knowledge that soon becomes obsolete is less valuable to acquire early. The simulations show how approximate dual control uses these differences to choose its experiments.  From information value to action selection Blackwell's comparison asks which decisions a given signal supports. Dual control makes signal acquisition part of the action: current knowledge determines what to try, and the result changes subsequent knowledge. Information-directed sampling focuses exploration on the unknown identity of the best action. The form of the dynamics model and the approximate planning method remain fixed. Beliefs and the actions chosen from them change.  Source and reading locations Edgar Klenske and Philipp Hennig (2016),  Dual Control for Approximate Bayesian Reinforcement Learning , JMLR 17(127). Paper page; full PDF. Checked: §3.1, equation (7), §4, and §6.1–6.5; experimental comparisons in Figures 1 and 5–7 and Table 1.", "paper": "Klenske & Hennig (2016). Dual Control for Approximate Bayesian Reinforcement Learning. JMLR 17(127)."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "information-directed-sampling", "title": "Information-directed sampling to find the best action", "category": "Knowledge and interaction", "text": "Information-directed sampling to find the best action A learner facing several options could investigate many unknowns, but only some affect which option is best. Russo and Van Roy's information-directed sampling focuses exploration on this question: how much immediate reward must the learner give up to obtain information about the optimal action?  Each experiment has a cost and an information gain Let A★ be the action with the highest expected reward in the unknown environment. Given the observations so far, the learner assigns probabilities to the possible identities of A★. These probabilities form its posterior distribution over the optimal action. For a candidate action a, Δₜ(a) is the expected reward lost by choosing a instead of A★, or expected regret. The quantity gₜ(a) is the expected reduction in uncertainty about A★ after observing the feedback from a, measured by mutual information. Both use the history available before step t. The algorithm may randomize its choice. If p(a) is the probability of choosing a, it minimizes the information ratio:   The numerator is squared expected regret; the denominator is expected information gain. The optimization is over the whole distribution p. Mixing actions can improve this trade-off, so the procedure is not equivalent to scoring each action separately and choosing the lowest score.  Why not resolve every uncertainty? Imagine a highly uncertain environmental parameter whose value never changes which action is best. Learning more about it increases knowledge of the world but does not identify A★. Conversely, a simple observation can be valuable if it rules out a strong but incorrect contender for the best action. The paper assumes a specified Bayesian environment model and a finite action set. The function mapping feedback to reward is known, and rewards are bounded; the environment's feedback distribution is unknown. Conditional on the environment, potential feedback is independent and identically distributed across rounds. Which feedback the learner actually observes still depends on its actions. The theory connects cumulative regret to the information ratio and the initial uncertainty about the optimal action. A uniform bound on the ratio controls cumulative regret. Experiments on Bernoulli and linear bandits compare cumulative regret with upper-confidence-bound methods, Thompson sampling, and knowledge-gradient methods.  Its place in the feedback process Dual control jointly considers present actions and future learning; information-directed sampling allocates exploration around the optimal action. Both infer within a specified environment model. Once informative feedback has been selected, the learner must still extract the relevant distinctions from it. Usable information studies that next step.  Source and reading locations Daniel Russo and Benjamin Van Roy (2014),  Learning to Optimize via Information-Directed Sampling , NeurIPS. Published full PDF. Checked: §2–4, equations (5)–(6), Proposition 2, and §6. The formula above expresses the information ratio over action distributions.", "paper": "Russo & Van Roy (2014). Learning to Optimize via Information-Directed Sampling. NeurIPS."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "usable-information", "title": "Why the same evidence is more usable for some learners", "category": "Knowledge and interaction", "text": "Why the same evidence is more usable for some learners A pattern can be present in data without being accessible to every model. As a simple illustration, let two independent inputs each be equally likely to equal +1 or −1, with their product determining the outcome. A predictor restricted to a linear readout may perform very differently when given the two inputs directly or their product as a feature. The evidence is the same; its usability changes. Xu and colleagues formalize this distinction as usable information.  Specify who is using the information Let X be the input, Y the outcome to predict, and 𝒱 the allowed family of predictive models. After observing X, a model f assigns probabilities to the possible values of Y. The expression f(X)(Y) is the probability it assigns to the outcome that actually occurs. The paper evaluates predictions by log loss. Repeatedly assigning low probability to observed outcomes incurs a large loss. The lowest average loss achievable within the family defines conditional V-entropy:   The infimum is the lowest loss that the family can approach; the expectation averages over the data distribution. Let H𝒱(Y) be the corresponding best loss without observing X. Their difference is the usable information supplied by the input:   The difference asks how much access to the input can improve prediction for this family. If the family allows a predictor to ignore the input, the old best prediction remains available, so the difference is nonnegative.  How computation can make information more usable Deterministic processing of an input cannot increase its Shannon mutual information with the prediction target. Yet the product example shows how processing can put the same pattern into a form that a restricted predictor can use. No new evidence is introduced. The paper compares information estimators on tasks including synthetic tree-structure recovery. Recovering a structure requires inferring relationships between variables, so how an estimator measures information in those relationships affects the recovered tree.  Available information and completed learning The definition uses the best prediction within a model family. A particular training run may not have found that predictor. The gap between current loss and the family optimum concerns training progress; the improvement made possible by a stronger family concerns predictive capacity. The merged-staircase property studies how training can acquire new features. Compression progress compares actual performance before and after an update. They address how usable information can be learned and how much learning has already changed performance.  Source and reading locations Yilun Xu and colleagues (2020),  A Theory of Usable Information under Computational Constraints , ICLR. Version used: arXiv v1; full text. Checked: Definitions 1–3, Proposition 2, §3.2, Theorem 1, and §6.1.", "paper": "Xu et al. (2020). A Theory of Usable Information under Computational Constraints. ICLR; v1."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "merged-staircase", "title": "How simple features create a path to further learning", "category": "Knowledge and interaction", "text": "How simple features create a path to further learning If a target contains a pattern only in the joint interaction of three variables, examining them one at a time may reveal nothing. A target that also contains single-variable and two-variable patterns offers a route into the more complex interaction. Abbe and colleagues study this learning path, beyond whether a network can represent the target at all.  One target offers a starting point The paper compares two functions whose input variables are independent and equally likely to take the values +1 and −1:   The first target contains x₁. That variable alone is correlated with the output, and a learned feature can help training detect subsequent product terms. The second target contains only the three-variable product. Neither individual variables nor two-variable products correlate with it, so the same low-order signal is absent.  The merged-staircase condition Write the target as a collection of nonzero product terms, and let Sₖ be the set of variables in term k. The merged-staircase property requires an ordering in which each new term introduces at most one previously unseen variable:   The union contains all variables used by earlier terms. The set difference leaves the new variables, and the bars count them. A term may reuse variables from several earlier terms, making this condition more general than growth along a single chain of products.  What the learning result assumes The authors consider sparse targets depending on a small number of input coordinates, two-layer networks, and batch stochastic gradient training at a specified scaling. Their analysis uses the mean-field limit: the evolving distribution of parameters approximates training in a wide network. The sample budget scales linearly with the input dimension. Under these training and resource conditions, a learnable target must have the merged-staircase property. Conversely, targets with this structure are learnable for almost all coefficient choices, although some exceptional combinations fall outside the guarantee. Experiments track the order in which different terms are learned and show degeneracies caused by special coefficient combinations. The learning difficulties here depend on the specified algorithm and resources.  From predictive capacity to a learning path Usable information concerns the best prediction available within a model family. The merged-staircase analysis asks whether training has a route to reach it. Existing features can change the starting point for later learning even when the architecture and update algorithm remain fixed.  Source and reading locations Emmanuel Abbe, Enric Boix-Adserà, and Theodor Misiakiewicz (2022),  The merged-staircase property: a necessary and nearly sufficient condition for SGD learning of sparse functions on two-layer neural networks , COLT. Paper page; full PDF. Checked: the motivating example on page 3, §1.1, §4, and Figure 2.", "paper": "Abbe et al. (2022). The merged-staircase property: a necessary and nearly sufficient condition for SGD learning of sparse functions on two-layer neural networks. COLT."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "compression-progress", "title": "Choosing experience through compression progress", "category": "Knowledge and interaction", "text": "Choosing experience through compression progress Revisiting a familiar pattern usually teaches little, while observing pure noise may never reveal a pattern. What should a learner seek between these extremes? Schmidhuber proposes seeking experiences that produce learning progress, measured by improvements in compression.  Learning a pattern can shorten a description A compressor that discovers a pattern can describe data through the pattern and its exceptions, rather than recording every detail separately. Learning can therefore be assessed by comparing the cost of encoding the same history before and after an update. Let h be a fixed history, C old and C new the compressors before and after learning, and L(C,h) the cost of encoding h with compressor C. A progress reward can be written as:   A positive difference means that the new compressor describes the same experiences at lower cost. Both must be evaluated on the same history: comparing an old compressor on difficult data with a new one on easy data would not isolate learning.  Using progress to choose the next action The system gives this reward to a controller that selects actions. The controller seeks experiences likely to improve compression. After the compressor learns from them, the measured improvement provides feedback to the controller. Learning thus changes where the system goes and what it observes; those observations, in turn, change what it learns. Familiar data may be easy to compress but offer no further progress. Pure noise keeps changing but usually supplies no stable, reusable patterns. The reward is intended to favor material that the learner has not mastered but can potentially learn. Progress is relative to the current learner. Structured data may produce no progress signal when compressor training fails or its capacity is insufficient. The reward measures the observed change between two versions.  How this differs from other information measures Usable information asks how much observing an input can improve prediction within a model family. Epiplexity characterizes the structure needed to describe data under a computational constraint. Compression progress compares old and new compressors on the same history, using completed learning to guide the choice of future experience. The article develops the framework and formalization and reviews existing implementations. The loop can operate with a fixed compressor update rule.  Source and reading locations Jürgen Schmidhuber (2008 draft, revised 2009),  Driven by Compression Progress: A Simple Principle Explains Essential Aspects of Subjective Beauty, Novelty, Surprise, Interestingness, Attention, Curiosity, Creativity, Art, Science, Music, Jokes . Version used: arXiv v2; full text. Checked: §1.2–1.3, §3, and Appendices A.5–A.7, especially equations (5)–(6).", "paper": "Schmidhuber (2009). Driven by Compression Progress: A Simple Principle Explains Essential Aspects of Subjective Beauty, Novelty, Surprise, Interestingness, Attention, Curiosity, Creativity, Art, Science, Music, Jokes. arXiv v2."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "epiplexity", "title": "Epiplexity and structure accessible under limited computation", "category": "Knowledge and interaction", "text": "Epiplexity and structure accessible under limited computation Data can be difficult to predict because it is close to random or because it contains complex patterns the learner has not understood. Both can produce large prediction errors, but they offer different learning opportunities. Finzi and colleagues introduce epiplexity through a computationally constrained coding problem that separates describable structure from the remaining unpredictable content.  Describing both the model and what it leaves unexplained Choose a probability model P, then use it to encode data X. The total cost has two parts: the bits needed to specify the program for P, and the bits still needed to encode the data using P. Among programs satisfying a runtime limit, the paper selects one minimizing the total:   Here 𝒫ₜ contains programs that can perform the required sampling and probability calculations within time T. The term  P  is the program's description length; the expectation is the average remaining data-encoding cost. P★ is a program achieving the best total code. Epiplexity is the first component,  P★ ; the associated time-bounded entropy is the second. Program length measures the cost of specifying a model. The runtime constraint determines which models are eligible.  Why structure differs from pure noise For random data without exploitable patterns, a complex program may not reduce the remaining code enough to justify its description cost. For some structured data, a longer program can capture many regularities and shorten the total description. Epiplexity measures the part of this optimal description allocated to the model, rather than the entire prediction error. The quantity depends on the computational budget and coding scheme. Relaxing the constraint can turn content previously recorded as a remainder into structure that a program can explain.  Testing whether the structure helps a task The authors arrange the same chess information into two pretraining sequences: board then moves, or moves then board. The first ordering has higher estimated epiplexity. After fine-tuning, the resulting models perform similarly on chess puzzles, while the first is better at predicting numerical position evaluations. This comparison shows that the organization of the same information affects what a bounded learner can extract, with benefits that depend on the downstream task. Experimental epiplexity is estimated approximately through training and coding.  Structure and progress are different quantities Compression progress measures how much an update reduces coding cost. Epiplexity characterizes the structural part of the best description under a constraint. V-information measures how much an input helps with a specified prediction target.  Source and reading locations Marc Finzi and colleagues (2026),  From Entropy to Epiplexity: Rethinking Information for Computationally Bounded Intelligence , preprint. Version used: arXiv v2; full PDF. Checked: Definitions 7–8 and Theorems 9–10 in §3, §4, Figure 7 in §6.1, and Appendix C.4.", "paper": "Finzi et al. (2026). From Entropy to Epiplexity: Rethinking Information for Computationally Bounded Intelligence. 预印本 v2."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "toolformer", "title": "How Toolformer learns to use existing tools", "category": "Knowledge and interaction", "text": "How Toolformer learns to use existing tools For an arithmetic problem, a language model can answer directly or call a calculator first. Toolformer lets the model generate its own training examples to learn which tool to call, when to call it, how to prepare its input, and how to use the result.  The model proposes calls and a filter tests their usefulness The researchers provide tool interfaces and a few examples. The model inserts candidate calls into ordinary text. The system executes them, puts their results back into the text, and checks whether they reduce the prediction error for subsequent words. Examples that pass this filter enter the fine-tuning data, teaching the model those uses. The positions of tool calls need no individual human annotation. Researchers specify the tools, initial examples, and loss-based filter. Fine-tuning changes the model's calling behavior; the tool implementations and training-example generation process remain fixed.  Separating the effects of training and execution One control is trained on the same corpus without tool calls, testing the effect of additional text training. Another disables tool calls at test time, testing the effect of executing a tool. On arithmetic tasks, the version allowed to call a calculator outperforms the version with calls disabled. Both retain the same trained parameters; the difference is whether the calculator can actually run. Some of the gain therefore comes from tool execution.  Tools can supply facts or perform calculations A lookup tool can provide facts absent from the input, while a calculator can evaluate an expression already given. Toolformer learns when to delegate these operations to existing interfaces. Voyager instead saves action programs it has written as callable skills: Toolformer learns to use existing tools, while Voyager adds reusable tool content.  Source and reading guide Schick et al.,  Toolformer: Language Models Can Teach Themselves to Use Tools , NeurIPS 2023. This account uses the arXiv v1 full text: §2 for the method, §4.1 and Table 4 for experiments. The focus is the generation, execution, and filtering of tool-call examples, and the controls using the same training corpus or disabling tool calls.", "paper": "Schick et al. (2023). Toolformer: Language Models Can Teach Themselves to Use Tools. NeurIPS; 核对v1."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "voyager", "title": "How Voyager turns accumulated skills into resources for later learning", "category": "Knowledge and interaction", "text": "How Voyager turns accumulated skills into resources for later learning After succeeding at a task, can an agent reuse the steps it has already solved on a related task? Voyager saves successful action programs in a skill library in Minecraft, where they can later be retrieved, called, and combined.  Turning execution into a reusable skill The system selects tasks based on the environment state and its existing abilities, then asks a language model to write code for executing them. The environment returns state changes and execution errors, which guide code revision. A separate language-model-based self-verification step judges whether the task is complete. Successful code enters the skill library. For a new task, the system retrieves relevant skills and gives them to the language model, allowing new code to focus on differences between tasks and on skill combinations. The base language model's weights remain frozen. The rules for task selection, retrieval, writing to memory, and self-verification also stay fixed; skill content accumulates.  What removing the skill library tests The authors remove the skill library to test whether task selection and code generation within the current attempt can sustain exploration. Exploration stalls earlier in this version, supporting the contribution of storing and calling earlier skills. The paper also tests skill transfer to tasks in a new world, extending evaluation beyond the original successful experience.  Skills and world models support later rounds in different ways Toolformer learns when to call supplied interfaces. Voyager adds its own programs to the set of callable tools. Earlier skills enter later combinations, allowing learning outcomes to support subsequent action directly. Memento 3 stores environmental rules for predicting actions not yet taken. A skill program provides steps for completing a task; a world model predicts an action's consequences. They accumulate reusable procedures and understanding of the environment, respectively.  Source and reading guide Wang et al.,  Voyager: An Open-Ended Embodied Agent with Large Language Models , first released in 2023, TMLR 2024. This account uses the arXiv v2 full text: §§2.1–2.3 for the curriculum, skill library, and feedback process; §§3.2–3.4 and Figures 1 and 9 for comparisons.", "paper": "Wang et al. (2024; 初稿2023). Voyager: An Open-Ended Embodied Agent with Large Language Models. TMLR; 核对v2."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "memento-3", "title": "How Memento 3 uses a world model to improve the next interaction", "category": "Knowledge and interaction", "text": "How Memento 3 uses a world model to improve the next interaction When exploring an unfamiliar game, a system must choose its next action after trying only a few possibilities. Memento 3 extracts environmental rules from experience to predict the outcomes of actions it has not yet tried. New execution results then drive revisions to those rules.  Turning experience into rules and an executable model The system extracts natural-language rules from interaction records to form a rulebook, then converts them into an executable world model. Given a state and an action, the model predicts the outcome. A planner uses those predictions to select an action for execution in the real environment. When the result differs from the prediction, the system revises the rules and their implementation. Two checks examine whether the program departs from the written rules and whether it reproduces state changes in past records. The rulebook and executable representation change across rounds; the base language model, revision process, and validation procedures remain fixed.  Can a rulebook reduce external trial and error? The authors remove the rulebook as a control while keeping the execution framework, validators, base model, reasoning effort, and other conditions the same. In the selected ablation games, both systems complete the tasks, but the version retaining the rulebook uses fewer environment actions. This supports the rulebook's contribution to interaction efficiency. Game examples further show how particular rules change in response to feedback.  Moving from agreement with history to new evidence Two models can explain past experience equally well but predict different outcomes for an untried action. Taking an action that distinguishes those predictions provides an opportunity to discover which explanation is wrong. Committing to one model too early can also steer planning away from such actions. This connects to model criticism: revise an explanation using existing records, then test it against new evidence. Memento 3 implements the first step as an executable rule-revision process and uses the revised model in the next round of action.  Source and reading guide Zhao et al.,  Memento 3: Model-Based Recursive Self-Improvement through Reflective Rulebooks , 2026 preprint. This account uses the arXiv v1 full text: §2 for the rulebook and validation process; §4, Tables 2–3 for games and the rulebook ablation; §5 for examples; and §1 for the discussion of historical consistency and exploration blind spots.", "paper": "Zhao et al. (2026). Memento 3: Model-Based Recursive Self-Improvement through Reflective Rulebooks. 预印本 v1."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "bayesian-model-criticism", "title": "Why posterior updating cannot replace model criticism", "category": "Knowledge and interaction", "text": "Why posterior updating cannot replace model criticism If data can only be explained with a straight line, adjusting the slope and intercept may find a better line but will not automatically produce a curve. This illustration captures the distinction emphasized by Gelman and Shalizi: choosing parameters within a model and discovering that the model is inadequate are different tasks.  Posterior updating reweights existing explanations Let D be the observed data and θ the model parameters. Bayesian updating combines the prior probabilities of parameters with how well they account for the data:   The prior p(θ) describes beliefs before observing the data. The likelihood p(D θ) measures how probable the data are under θ. The posterior p(θ D) describes beliefs afterward. The proportionality symbol means that a normalizing constant is needed to make the total probability one. This comparison stays within the specified model. If an important variable or relationship is missing, more data may concentrate the posterior around the best-fitting parameters without testing whether the model family is adequate. In the line example, greater certainty about a slope does not remove a curved pattern in the data.  Predictive checks look for what the model misses Model criticism asks what features the data should exhibit if the current model were adequate. It then compares predictions with observations to find systematic discrepancies. For example, prediction errors that vary systematically with the input can suggest a missing relationship rather than merely imprecisely estimated parameters. Researchers can respond by choosing different variables, changing the functional form, or revising assumptions about data generation, then fitting and checking again. Posterior computation learns within the current model; predictive checks and revisions change the explanations available for subsequent learning.  Why evidence selection matters for self-improvement Memento 3 writes experience into revisable rules and encounters a related distinction: new feedback may require adjusting an existing rule or rewriting the rule itself. Which errors a check can reveal also depends on the available data. Two models may predict past experience equally well while disagreeing about actions never tried. A system that always acts according to one model may never encounter evidence that distinguishes them. Model revision therefore requires both using existing counterexamples and taking actions that can expose new ones.  Source and reading locations Gelman and Shalizi,  Philosophy and the practice of Bayesian statistics , published in 2013. This note uses the authors' arXiv v3 full text, particularly §2, §4.1, and §4.3. The article is a methodological analysis of statistical modeling.", "paper": "Gelman & Shalizi (2013). Philosophy and the practice of Bayesian statistics. BJMSP 66:8–38; 核对作者v3."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "learned-optimizers", "title": "Learned Optimizers: Learning How to Update Another Model", "category": "Learning mechanisms", "text": "Learned Optimizers: Learning How to Update Another Model A gradient describes how a small parameter change affects the loss; an optimizer must still choose the direction and magnitude of each update. Andrychowicz and colleagues train another network to learn this update rule, using gradient history to guide the next training step.  One model learns to update another Let θ denote the task model’s parameters and φ the optimizer’s parameters. The optimizer reads task gradients and its historical state, then outputs an update to add to θ. To train the optimizer, the researchers run it for multiple steps on a collection of tasks and adjust φ using the task losses along those trajectories. Changes to θ train the task model; changes to φ train the optimizer to perform those updates. On a new test task, φ is fixed, while the task parameters and optimizer state continue to change. The outer backpropagation procedure, optimization algorithm, and task distribution are specified by the researchers.  Comparing update rules and their transfer The authors train or test optimizers on random quadratic functions, image classification, and neural style transfer, comparing them with conventional methods whose learning rates have been tuned. Replacing the update rule on the same task tests the effectiveness of the learned optimizer. The paper also varies the number of optimization steps or the task model’s architecture to test whether the update rule remains useful outside its training setting. Evaluation thus covers both optimization on the training problems and transfer across settings.  Three ways to learn a learning mechanism Learned optimizers acquire a rule for updating model parameters. Neural architecture search learns which model structures to propose. Meta-reinforcement learning allows a network with fixed weights to adapt through its internal state. These approaches use experience to shape update rules, architecture selection, and state changes, respectively.  Source and reading guide Andrychowicz et al. (2016),  Learning to learn by gradient descent by gradient descent , NeurIPS; arXiv v2. See §2, Equations (2)–(3), §3, and the transfer experiments. Paper · Full text", "paper": "Andrychowicz et al. (2016). Learning to learn by gradient descent by gradient descent. NeurIPS; v2."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "neural-architecture-search", "title": "Neural Architecture Search: Learning to Propose Better Models", "category": "Learning mechanisms", "text": "Neural Architecture Search: Learning to Propose Better Models Trying many architectures at random can also produce a good network. Zoph and Le train an architecture controller to test whether feedback from previously trained candidates helps it propose better networks.  How the controller uses candidate performance The controller generates symbols describing a network, such as layer settings or connections within a recurrent cell. The system builds the candidate, trains its weights, and evaluates it on validation data. The controller uses this evaluation as a reward and learns, through reinforcement learning, to adjust the probabilities of proposing different architectures. Each round therefore contains two learning processes: candidate networks learn the task, while the controller learns to propose networks. The former updates weights; the latter updates the distribution over architecture proposals.  What the random-search comparison supports The authors search for convolutional architectures in image classification and recurrent cells in language modeling. The language-modeling experiment compares controller-based search with random search, examining both the best candidate and the average performance of leading candidates. The controller outperforms random search in these comparisons, supporting the use of training feedback to improve subsequent proposals. Performance across several strong candidates provides evidence beyond a single best result; it does not require every proposal to outperform its predecessor.  Architecture search and update rules The controller learns within a search space specified by the designers; its own architecture and update algorithm remain fixed. Learned optimizers change how a given model is trained, while architecture search changes which model is proposed. Both turn a component of producing task capability into a mechanism that can learn from experience.  Source and reading guide Zoph and Le (2017),  Neural Architecture Search with Reinforcement Learning , ICLR; arXiv v2. See §3.1–3.2, §4.1–4.2, and the random-search comparison. Paper · Full text", "paper": "Zoph & Le (2017). Neural Architecture Search with Reinforcement Learning. ICLR; v2."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "meta-reinforcement-learning", "title": "Meta-Reinforcement Learning: Adaptation with Fixed Weights", "category": "Learning mechanisms", "text": "Meta-Reinforcement Learning: Adaptation with Fixed Weights A network with fixed weights can still adjust its actions after a recent success or failure. Wang and colleagues locate this rapid adaptation in a recurrent network’s internal state: weights encode a learned way of adapting, while state changes with experience in the current task.  Training across tasks and adapting within a task During training, the network repeatedly encounters related tasks. After acting, it receives rewards and other interaction information, updates its internal state, and selects its next action. Outer reinforcement learning adjusts the weights to improve returns across the interaction sequence, thereby shaping how state responds to feedback. On a new test task, weights remain fixed while the network continues to receive feedback, update its state, and act. For example, when returns change, recent experience can alter its choices. Rapid adaptation thus occurs through state changes without retraining the weights.  Testing how the network adapts The paper uses tasks with correlated options, fluctuating returns, and two-stage choices. In volatile environments, the authors compare the network with classical exploration strategies and fixed-learning-rate rules. They also analyze its behavior to estimate the weight it places on recent feedback. These tasks make a fixed action inadequate over time. Behavioral analysis examines whether adaptation changes with environmental volatility. The experiments therefore assess both task performance and the way feedback is used.  Learning through state and through parameters At test time, new experiences change the state; how those experiences are retained and used has already been learned during outer training. Learned optimizers explicitly produce updates to another model’s parameters, whereas this approach adapts through the task network’s state. Both make a learning method the product of learning, with the outer training process still specified by the researchers.  Source and reading guide Wang et al.,  Learning to reinforcement learn . First preprint 2016; this account uses arXiv v3 from 2017. See §2.3, §3.1.3–3.1.4, §3.2, and the behavioral analyses. Paper · Full text", "paper": "Wang et al. (2016/2017). Learning to reinforcement learn. arXiv:1611.05763v3."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "stop", "title": "How STOP evaluates a program that produces improvements", "category": "Learning mechanisms", "text": "How STOP evaluates a program that produces improvements Can a program that edits code become better at editing code by rewriting itself? STOP distinguishes a task program from an improvement program. The task program solves a problem; the improvement program takes task code and a scoring function, then calls a language model to propose changes. Its value is measured by how well the modified task programs perform.  Evaluating self-modification through downstream performance The researchers first use the same improvement program to modify a set of task programs and calculate their average utility after modification. During self-modification, the current improvement program edits its own source code to produce a new version. That version receives the same downstream evaluation. Better-performing versions can take over subsequent self-modification. The base language model, task utilities, and budget mechanism remain researcher-specified. Recursion occurs in the code that organizes language-model calls, and evaluation depends on the changes that code actually produces.  Testing whether the improvement program becomes more useful One task is learning parity with noisy labels: the task program must learn a parity relation among bits from examples whose labels may be wrong. The authors compare the self-modified improvement program with the initial program, single-pass generation, and greedy iteration. They evaluate the resulting task programs on independent test instances. Experiments on other tasks examine whether the modification methods transfer. Self-modification improves average performance with some base models, but can reduce it with weaker models. The experiments therefore support recursive improvement under particular model and task conditions.  Connections to DGM and HyperAgents DGM uses one program for both problem solving and self-modification, retaining past versions for further search. STOP directly optimizes an improvement program through its downstream results. HyperAgents also distinguishes task and improvement programs and allows both to be edited. Its transfer experiments move both parts together and evaluate how the combined system helps on a new task.  Source and reading guide Zelikman, Lorch, Mackey, and Kalai,  Self-Taught Optimizer (STOP): Recursively Self-Improving Code Generation , COLM 2024. This account uses the arXiv v3 PDF: §§3–4 and Algorithm 1 for improvement programs and recursion; §§5.1–5.3, Figure 4, and Table 1 for task comparisons and transfer experiments.", "paper": "Zelikman et al. (2024). Self-Taught Optimizer (STOP): Recursively Self-Improving Code Generation. COLM; v3."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "darwin-godel-machine", "title": "How DGM lets descendant programs continue self-modification", "category": "Learning mechanisms", "text": "How DGM lets descendant programs continue self-modification After a coding agent modifies itself, can the new version help make further changes? The Darwin Gödel Machine (DGM) lets descendant programs participate in self-modification and retains several past versions for later search.  One program performs two jobs During task evaluation, the program fixes issues in code repositories or solves programming tasks. During self-modification, it reads a modification request and changes its own tools and workflow. The system selects a starting version from its history, makes changes, and evaluates the new version on programming tasks. Usable programs are retained as code in an archive. A version that is not currently best may still contain useful tools or procedures, so keeping it preserves a starting point for further changes. The base language model, the outer process that generates modification instructions, and the version-selection rule remain fixed. The program carrying out those instructions can be a descendant produced earlier.  Freezing the modifier and removing the archive One control always uses the initial program to make modifications, preventing descendants from taking over. Its later gains level off sooner, supporting the value of allowing descendants to participate in modification on the tested coding tasks. A second control removes the archive and continues only from the latest working version. Poor modifications more readily obstruct this process, while the full system can return to another branch. This comparison tests the value of preserving search paths, complementing the first comparison's test of changes to the modifier.  How coding ability can support self-modification DGM draws on the overlap between solving programming tasks and editing its own implementation. HyperAgents makes more of the improvement process editable and evaluates transfer across domains. DGM evaluates candidates through task experiments and keeps multiple branches for further search. The original Gödel machine requires a proof that a modification is beneficial. The two approaches use empirical evaluation and formal proof, respectively, as the basis for modification.  Source and reading guide Zhang, Hu, Lu, Lange, and Clune,  Darwin Gödel Machine: Open-Ended Evolution of Self-Improving Agents , first released in 2025. This account uses the arXiv v3 full text: §3 and Algorithms 1–3 for the procedure; §§4.1–4.4 and Appendices E.2–E.3 for tasks and ablations.", "paper": "Zhang et al. (2025/2026). Darwin Gödel Machine: Open-Ended Evolution of Self-Improving Agents. arXiv v3."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "hyperagents", "title": "How HyperAgents makes the improvement process editable", "category": "Learning mechanisms", "text": "How HyperAgents makes the improvement process editable A stronger task program does not necessarily imply a stronger program for modifying it. HyperAgents separates the task program from the improvement program and allows both to be edited. The system can change how it solves a task and how it searches for better solutions.  How the improvement program enters the next round The improvement program can edit task code as well as the code for diagnosing errors, proposing changes, and organizing later modifications. New versions undergo task evaluation and remain available for search. Their improvement programs can then perform the next round of modification. DGM already allows descendants to make modifications, while keeping the outer process that generates modification instructions fixed. HyperAgents exposes more of that process as editable code. The base language model, external task scoring, and version-selection rule in the main experiments remain fixed.  Testing improvement ability separately During evaluation, the improvement program is held fixed while it modifies a given task program and candidates already generated. A validation set selects a version, and a test set measures its performance relative to the starting task program. This tests how much improvement a particular version can produce. The number of candidates is limited, although attempts can still have different computational costs. The paper also freezes the improvement program, removes the historical archive, and compares with DGM to examine self-modification and the retention of search branches.  Transferring experience with modification The authors transfer systems developed on paper reviewing and robotics reward design to grading mathematical solutions. Under the same evaluation rules and candidate limit, the transferred versions produce larger improvements than a system starting with the initial task and improvement programs. The improvement program stays fixed during this evaluation. Both parts of the code transfer together, so the result supports the usefulness of the combined system on the new task. Improvements also include repairing output formatting so that the grading process runs correctly; they cannot all be attributed to the improvement program or to mathematical understanding. STOP directly optimizes an improvement program through its downstream modifications. HyperAgents includes both task and improvement code in the search and evaluates the effects of transferring them together.  Source and reading guide Zhang et al.,  Hyperagents , 2026 preprint. This account uses the arXiv v1 full text: §§3–5, especially the improvement-ability evaluation in §5.2. Appendices C.4, D.3–D.4, E.3, and E.5 provide scoring, candidate-selection, and system details.", "paper": "Zhang et al. (2026). Hyperagents. arXiv:2603.19461v1，预印本."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "plasticity", "title": "Why continual learning also requires plasticity", "category": "Learning mechanisms", "text": "Why continual learning also requires plasticity Does a network remain able to learn new tasks as training continues? Dohare and colleagues find that accumulated training can make later problems harder to learn. The ability to keep learning from new experience is called plasticity.  Failing to learn new tasks and forgetting old ones Forgetting research asks how much old knowledge survives after learning a new task. Plasticity research asks how well the network can still learn the new task. The paper trains networks on a sequence of changing tasks and evaluates each one. Performance on later tasks can deteriorate even when retaining earlier tasks is not required.  Resetting low-utility units to preserve learning ability In settings including sequences of image tasks, the authors compare standard backpropagation, regularization or perturbation methods, and continual backpropagation. Alongside gradient updates, continual backpropagation tracks units' utility. Units that have trained for some time but still contribute little can be reset, giving subsequent learning room to adjust. The groups face the same task sequences. In the tested sequences, resetting units reduces the decline in later performance. This indicates that changes caused by prior training contribute to the decline, and that changing the update method can help preserve learning ability. The rules for selecting and resetting units are designed by the researchers and remain fixed during training.  Feedback must still be learnable after it arrives A system can receive new feedback while becoming less able to turn it into capability. Plasticity research addresses this failure, separately from a lack of corrective evidence. Learned optimizers use another model to learn parameter-update rules, while meta-reinforcement learning uses training across tasks to shape adaptation through internal state. These lines change how a system updates and adapts; maintaining plasticity helps it continue to use later learning opportunities.  Source and reading guide The journal article is Dohare et al.,  Loss of plasticity in deep continual learning , Nature, 2024. The method description here follows the authors' preprint v3, titled  Maintaining Plasticity in Deep Continual Learning . Its title and some of its organization differ from the journal version. The relevant sections are §§2 and 5, Algorithm 1, and the sequential-task experiments. Journal record · Author version used · PDF", "paper": "Dohare et al. (2024). Loss of plasticity in deep continual learning. Nature 632:768–774; 方法核对作者v3."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "self-confirming-equilibrium", "title": "Self-confirming equilibrium: why accurate feedback can leave mistaken beliefs intact", "category": "Feedback and learning dynamics", "text": "Self-confirming equilibrium: why accurate feedback can leave mistaken beliefs intact Real feedback can correct errors that become visible, while leaving unchosen alternatives untested. Fudenberg and Levine use  self-confirming equilibrium  to describe a state in which an agent predicts the consequences of its own behavior correctly, yet may choose an inferior action because it misunderstands the alternatives.  How an untried option stays undervalued Consider an illustrative example. A seller expects a new product to fail and therefore sells only the old product. The seller accurately predicts sales of the old product but never obtains sales data for the new one. The new product might be more profitable, yet the feedback received each day cannot reveal this. Two conditions hold: the old product is best under the seller's beliefs, and those beliefs correctly explain observed sales. Behavior follows the seller's judgment, and observations give no reason to revise it. Self-confirmation can therefore occur without exaggerating current returns. Errors about untried options allow accurate predictions along the chosen path to coexist with inferior decisions.  From the example to the definition The paper studies finite extensive-form games, which describe the sequence of actions and allow players to observe only part of the history. A player's plan determines which situations it actually encounters. The definition requires an adopted strategy to be optimal under some belief, with that belief correct at the information sets actually reached. An information set groups decision situations that the player cannot distinguish from its observations; unreached information sets face no equivalent test. Different pure strategies played with positive probability may be supported by different beliefs. The definition describes how behavior and observed feedback sustain one another. Whether learning converges to such a state is a separate question.  How errors escape testing Beliefs lead an agent to avoid certain actions, and those actions then produce no counterevidence. Correction depends on which feedback has a chance to appear. Berk–Nash equilibrium also handles model misspecification: every model an agent considers may omit a regularity, so even observed data can be explained only approximately. For RSI, exploration changes the evidence available; expanding the model class changes the explanations available.  Source and reading guide Fudenberg and Levine, 1993,  Self-Confirming Equilibrium , Econometrica 61, 523–545. Published paper PDF. See §3, Definition 1, p. 527, for the definition; pp. 523 and 528 for motivation and discussion.", "paper": "Fudenberg & Levine (1993). Self-Confirming Equilibrium. Econometrica 61:523–545."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "berk-nash", "title": "Berk–Nash equilibrium: how feedback can sustain a mistaken view", "category": "Feedback and learning dynamics", "text": "Berk–Nash equilibrium: how feedback can sustain a mistaken view A learner searches its existing models for the explanation that best fits experience. Its actions, in turn, determine which experiences it collects. Esponda and Pouzo's Berk–Nash equilibrium connects these two steps: beliefs best fit the feedback received, and actions are optimal under those beliefs. The two can support each other while remaining at odds with the real world.  A two-action example Consider an illustrative example. A learner chooses A or B, receiving 1 for success and 0 for failure. The true success probabilities are 0.6 for A and 0.8 for B, so B is better. The learner considers only models in which A succeeds with probability θ and B with probability θ/2, where θ can be 0.4, 0.6, or 0.8. Every model ranks B below A. None can represent the true world. If the learner always chooses A, long-run feedback supports θ = 0.6. This parameter correctly predicts A but assigns B a success probability of 0.3. The learner therefore keeps choosing A, and those choices keep producing evidence for θ = 0.6. Trying B would expose the mistake. Yet as long as the model requires B's success probability to be half of A's, no parameter can explain both actions correctly. Exploration changes the evidence; changing the model class changes the available explanations.  The best fit to experience Start with one learner, finitely many actions and outcomes, and a fixed payoff function. Let a denote an action, y the feedback, and u(a,y) the payoff. The world generates feedback according to Q W(y∣a). The learner considers models Q θ(y∣a), indexed by a parameter set Θ. The frequency of choosing action a is ρ(a). Frequently chosen actions receive more weight when assessing fit. The weighted KL divergence K measures prediction error, and Θ (ρ) collects the parameters that minimize it:   KL measures the extra expected log loss from predicting with the subjective model rather than the true distribution. An action never chosen has zero weight, so the data do not test it directly. Structural assumptions can still constrain its prediction: in the example, A and B share the same parameter. With a fixed model class, stable action frequencies, and the relevant regularity conditions, Bayesian updating concentrates posterior probability on the best-fitting models. Differences in long-run average log likelihood correspond to differences in weighted KL divergence.  Best-fitting beliefs must also support the actions The learner may believe several models are possible. Let b be its probability distribution over θ. Its prediction for feedback is the corresponding mixture:   A single-agent Berk–Nash equilibrium requires the strategy ρ and belief b to satisfy two conditions. First, all belief probability falls on the models that best fit the data generated by the current actions:   Second, every action actually used maximizes expected payoff under that mixture belief:   The first condition connects feedback to beliefs; the second connects beliefs back to actions. In the example, b concentrates on θ = 0.6, and A is optimal under that belief, so both conditions hold. The paper also allows multiple players and private signals, with each player's feedback distribution affected by the others' strategies.  Self-confirmation, Nash equilibrium, and learning convergence Self-confirming equilibrium allows mistakes off the observed path: beliefs correctly predict observed feedback but misjudge untried actions. Berk–Nash also allows errors on the observed path because the true distribution may lie outside the model class. When the model is correctly specified, Berk–Nash connects to self-confirming equilibrium. Adding strong identification, so that best-fitting models agree on predictions for every action, recovers the corresponding Nash conclusion. This rules out models that agree on observed evidence but disagree about untried actions. Equilibrium describes mutually supporting beliefs and actions; convergence asks whether learning reaches them. The paper fixes the subjective model class, uses Bayesian updating, and studies games with small payoff perturbations. Theorem 2 shows that strategies which stabilize under the relevant optimal policies must satisfy the Berk–Nash conditions of the perturbed game. The converse needs additional conditions. The 2019 correction requires weak identification at the equilibrium, a belief supporting it, and a prior with a specified conditional distribution on the best-fitting set; policies may have vanishing optimization errors. Endogenous misspecified learning further examines which equilibria can become long-run limits of action.  Reading GANs and model collapse through feedback Comparing the feedback processes, Berk–Nash studies how actions select evidence and misspecified models interpret it. Finite-discriminator GAN theory studies which differences an evaluator can detect once samples are available. Model collapse studies how errors propagate when one generation's outputs become the next generation's data. RSI can alter these steps through exploration, tools, and model revision. How feedback corrects a learner connects them and uses board games and code to show how effective feedback supports improvement.  Source and further reading - Ignacio Esponda and Demian Pouzo,  Berk–Nash Equilibrium: A Framework for Modeling Agents with Misspecified Models , Econometrica 84, 1093–1130, 2016. Published article. - This account uses arXiv v4, including the 2019 correction. Weighted KL and equilibrium: §2.3, Definition 1. Connections to Nash and self-confirming equilibrium: §3.2. Learning results: §4.2, Theorem 2, §4.3, and the correction. - Self-confirming equilibrium: why mistakes can persist without evidence from untried actions. - Endogenous misspecified learning: which self-consistent actions can become learning limits.", "paper": "Esponda & Pouzo (2016). Berk–Nash Equilibrium: A Framework for Modeling Agents with Misspecified Models. Econometrica; v4含2019勘误."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "endogenous-misspecified-learning", "title": "Endogenous misspecified learning: which self-consistent states can persist?", "category": "Feedback and learning dynamics", "text": "Endogenous misspecified learning: which self-consistent states can persist? Berk–Nash equilibrium allows a belief to support the current action, but the learner continues updating that belief. Fudenberg, Lanzani, and Strack ask which actions can remain unchanged as learning continues.  Equally good explanations can recommend different actions Several models can have the same average prediction loss while making different predictions and recommending different actions. New observations change the learner's confidence in these models, potentially changing its actions as well. Ordinary Berk–Nash equilibrium requires the existence of a belief that assigns probability only to best-fitting models and makes the current action optimal. For an action to remain stable through learning, we must also ask what happens if the learner comes to trust another equally good explanation.  A stable action must withstand changes in belief Let a  denote a candidate limiting action and Θ (a ) the models that best fit the feedback generated by repeatedly choosing it. Let μ be a belief over those models, and let rθ(a) be the expected payoff of action a under model θ. The paper gives the following necessary condition:   This is the  uniform Berk–Nash  condition: the original action remains optimal under every allocation of belief across the best-fitting models. Changes in their posterior weights therefore leave the action optimal. The result concerns Bayesian decision problems with a fixed subjective model class and finite sets of actions and outcomes. If optimal learning behavior converges, its limit must satisfy this condition. The paper also gives conditions under which strict equilibria can arise as long-run outcomes from suitable initial beliefs; convergence of general trajectories remains a separate question.  From current consistency to lasting stability Equilibrium explains why an agent keeps its current action. This paper asks why it can keep that action after further learning, as confidence shifts among its best-fitting explanations. For RSI, this provides a dynamic baseline with fixed learning mechanisms. Expanding the model class, changing observation tools, or modifying the update rule changes the available explanations or the learning process. Stability conditions must then be reconsidered.  Source and reading guide Fudenberg, Lanzani, and Strack, 2021,  Limit Points of Endogenous Misspecified Learning , Econometrica 89, 1065–1098. Published paper PDF. The introduction, especially pp. 1065–1067, presents the problem, definitions, and main conclusions of Theorems 1–2. The equation above illustrates the condition for convergence to a single action.", "paper": "Fudenberg, Lanzani & Strack (2021). Limit Points of Endogenous Misspecified Learning. Econometrica 89:1065–1098."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "performative-prediction", "title": "Performative prediction: how a model changes its next training data", "category": "Feedback and learning dynamics", "text": "Performative prediction: how a model changes its next training data Once a credit-scoring rule is deployed, applicants may adjust what they report, changing the next round of training data. Perdomo and colleagues study this form of performative prediction: deployment affects the environment, and retraining uses the data the environment returns.  Retraining and deployment optimization Let φ be the parameters of the deployed model and D(φ) the data distribution it induces. Let θ be the parameters of the model being evaluated, and ℓ(z; θ) its loss on sample z:   The second argument determines the data source; the first determines which model is scored. Retraining collects data induced by the old model θₜ, then selects a new model minimizing R(θ; θₜ). Deployment optimization instead minimizes R(θ; θ): when changing the model, it also accounts for the new data distribution that model will induce. If retraining stops at θ , then θ  is best suited to D(θ ). Another model may change people's behavior and achieve lower loss on the resulting distribution. Stability and optimal deployment can therefore differ.  When retraining converges The paper requires the loss gradient to be smooth in both parameters and samples: changes in θ or z must change the gradient at a rate bounded by β. The loss must also be γ-strongly convex in its parameters, giving optimization on the current distribution a unique, stable solution. Deployment-induced distribution changes are measured using Wasserstein-1 distance: the minimum average distance required to transport one distribution into another. A unit change in parameters may change the distribution by at most ε units. When ε < γ/β, repeated population risk minimization converges to a unique stable point. This bound gives a sufficient condition for stable retraining. “Population” means each round uses the exact distributional expectation; the finite-sample version additionally requires tail conditions and sample sizes that grow with iteration.  Bringing feedback into the objective Outside the Echo Chamber studies the gap between stability and optimality, estimating the environment's response to deployment in order to optimize performative risk. Berk–Nash examines how evidence selected by actions supports subjective beliefs. Performative prediction puts the model's effect on the data distribution directly into the objective. For RSI, evaluating a modification likewise requires accounting for its effects on subsequent feedback.  Source and reading guide Perdomo and colleagues, 2020,  Performative Prediction , ICML. Paper page and published PDF. Risk and stability are defined in §2. Convergence conditions appear in Theorem 3.5; related distinctions appear in Proposition 3.6 and §4.", "paper": "Perdomo et al. (2020). Performative Prediction. ICML."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "outside-echo-chamber", "title": "Outside the Echo Chamber: optimizing performance after deployment feedback", "category": "Feedback and learning dynamics", "text": "Outside the Echo Chamber: optimizing performance after deployment feedback A model can stop changing after repeated retraining on the data it induces, even though a better deployment remains possible. Miller, Perdomo, and Zrnic first learn how the environment responds to a model, then incorporate that response into optimization.  Why a stable model can still be suboptimal In the performative prediction setting, θ denotes the deployed parameters, D(θ) the resulting data distribution, and ℓ(z; θ) the model's loss on a sample z. Performative risk is   Changing θ changes both the loss calculation and the source of data. Repeated retraining optimizes only the first part: each round holds the existing distribution fixed and finds the best parameters for it. At a fixed point, the current model is best for the current data, but optimization may have missed the benefit of inducing different data with another model. The paper proves that a stable point can have substantially higher performative risk than the optimum. Even if the loss is convex in the parameters, PR need not be convex because D(θ) changes too. The authors impose structure on the distributional response to analyze the risk and develop optimization methods.  Learn the response, then optimize deployment In settings such as location families, the change in data can be described by a shift in the distribution's location. The authors deploy models, collect data, estimate how the parameters move the distribution, and use that estimate to optimize performative risk. Deployment thus supplies information about environmental responses for the next decision. Experiments use synthetic linear regression with deployment effects and a credit-scoring simulator in which applicants adjust manipulable features. The authors compare the two-stage method with derivative-free optimization and stochastic-gradient methods with different update frequencies, tracking risk against the number of samples collected. The two-stage method optimizes performative risk more effectively in these settings. The experiments also include stronger feedback outside the regime covered by the theoretical guarantees.  How to leave the echo chamber Here, the “echo chamber” arises when a model repeatedly uses data it causes without accounting for how it causes those data to change. Estimating the environmental response lets optimization consider the consequences of a different deployment. Berk–Nash examines which beliefs can support an action; this paper examines whether optimization accounts for an action's effect on future data. For RSI, the latter question extends the evaluation of a modification to the feedback it subsequently causes.  Source and reading guide Miller, Perdomo, and Zrnic, 2021,  Outside the Echo Chamber: Optimizing the Performative Risk , ICML. Paper page and published PDF. See §1.1 for the distinction between stability and optimality; §§3–5 and Figures 1–2 for theory, algorithms, and experiments.", "paper": "Miller, Perdomo & Zrnic (2021). Outside the Echo Chamber: Optimizing the Performative Risk. ICML."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "model-collapse", "title": "Model collapse: why learning only from the previous generation loses content", "category": "Feedback and learning dynamics", "text": "Model collapse: why learning only from the previous generation loses content If each generation learns only from its predecessor's samples, how long can rare content survive? Shumailov and colleagues study a repeated process: train a model, sample from it, and train the next model on those samples. Sampling and fitting errors also enter the next generation's data.  How one omission changes later data A rare event may be absent from a generation's finite sample. The next model sees only that sample and may assign the event an even lower probability, making it harder to generate later. An omission in one sample can thus change future sampling distributions. Fitting adds another source of error. Finite data and a given model class only approximate the target distribution, and this approximation becomes the new data source. Each generation inherits upstream bias and adds its own sampling and fitting errors.  Generational replacement with fixed sample size The discrete and Gaussian analyses consider a fixed number of samples generated solely by the preceding model at each generation. The discrete analysis shows how finite sampling can eliminate categories. In the Gaussian process analyzed, covariance tends to zero almost surely, while the expected squared Wasserstein-2 distance from the original distribution diverges. The squared distance measures the minimum average squared displacement needed to match the distributions. A narrowing distribution along a typical trajectory therefore need not become closer to the original distribution in expected distance.  Comparing data sources in language models The authors repeatedly fine-tune language models on generated text and compare this with continued inclusion of original data, examining changes in rare content and distributional detail. They also vary the repetition penalty used in generation to examine the effect of sampling choices. The experiments support retaining original data to mitigate degradation. The two main settings also differ in the number of passes through the training data at each generation—the number of epochs—so the comparison varies both data source and training amount.  Reading GANs and Berk–Nash through feedback Comparing the feedback processes, model collapse studies who generates the next round of data. Berk–Nash studies which world responses actions select and how subjective models interpret them. Finite-discriminator GAN theory studies which differences an evaluator can recognize once samples are available. Data accumulation changes error propagation by retaining original and earlier generated data. How feedback corrects a learner connects data sources, evidence selection, and discriminative capacity, using board games and code to show how effective feedback supports improvement.  Source and reading guide Shumailov and colleagues, 2024,  AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data , Nature 631, 755–759. Published full text. The process is defined in Definition 2.1. See Theoretical intuition and Theorem 3.1 for the analysis, and the experimental sections for language models and repetition penalties. Conditions for the purely synthetic process follow the 2025 author correction.", "paper": "Shumailov et al. (2024). AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data. Nature 631:755–759; 含2025修订."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "data-accumulation", "title": "Data accumulation: why retaining history changes self-training error", "category": "Feedback and learning dynamics", "text": "Data accumulation: why retaining history changes self-training error Should each generation replace its data with newly generated samples, or retain data from earlier generations? Gerstgrasser and colleagues change this training protocol to study how errors propagate in model collapse.  How history constrains later errors Under replacement, each new model depends only on data generated by its predecessor. A generation's bias enters the entire new dataset, while earlier information no longer participates directly in fitting. Accumulation retains the original data and adds samples from each generation, keeping later models constrained by that history. The paper compares the protocols exactly in linear regression. The original dataset has T samples with d-dimensional inputs. Each generation reuses their design matrix and generates new outputs, adding independent Gaussian noise with variance σ². The following formula assumes isotropic Gaussian inputs, a full-column-rank design matrix, and T ≥ d+2. Writing C = σ²d/(T−d−1), the expected excess test mean-squared error due to model estimation at generation n is:   Replacement adds an error contribution of the same order at each generation, so error grows with the number of generations. Under accumulation, later contributions receive diminishing weights, and their sum has a finite upper bound. Retaining history bounds the error rather than driving it to zero.  Comparisons in deep models The authors compare replacement and accumulation in language, molecular, and image generation. Language models are initialized afresh at each generation. A further replacement baseline increases dataset size across generations, testing whether gains merely reflect access to more samples. Accumulation mitigates degradation in several tasks, although image variational autoencoders can still lose detail. It also uses more samples and gradient steps. The language experiments use model-generated TinyStories as the original reference corpus: “original” here means the data retained from the start of the cycle.  Memory in the feedback process Accumulation makes existing information less vulnerable to being overwritten by later errors. For RSI, it addresses how past learning is preserved; new feedback, representation expansion, and update rules determine what can be learned next.  Source and reading guide Gerstgrasser and colleagues, 2024,  Is Model Collapse Inevitable? Breaking the Curse of Recursion by Accumulating Real and Synthetic Data . arXiv v2. Data protocols and experiments: §2.1–2.3. Linear regression: §3.1–3.2, Theorems 1–2. Experimental controls: Appendix C.", "paper": "Gerstgrasser et al. (2024). Is Model Collapse Inevitable? Breaking the Curse of Recursion by Accumulating Real and Synthetic Data. arXiv v2."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "reward-overoptimization", "title": "Reward overoptimization: why proxy scores rise while reference scores fall", "category": "Feedback and learning dynamics", "text": "Reward overoptimization: why proxy scores rise while reference scores fall A scoring model can help a system find better answers, yet steer it in the wrong direction when optimized repeatedly. Gao, Schulman, and Hilton ask how a fixed reference model evaluates outputs as a generation policy works harder to maximize a proxy score.  Optimization favors overvalued outputs The proxy score only approximates the target evaluation. Initially, raising the proxy score can also raise the reference score. As search intensifies, however, the system becomes more likely to find outputs that the proxy overvalues. If overvaluation puts an answer above its competitors, that answer becomes more likely to be selected. A consistently overvalued class of answers can then become a direction the policy favors. Small scoring errors on randomly sampled answers can produce larger distortions when selecting the highest-scoring answer. Optimization changes the output distribution and, with it, which evaluation errors the system encounters.  Use two evaluators to track divergence The experiment fixes a reference reward model, uses it to score answers, and trains a smaller proxy reward model on those labels. During optimization, the generation system receives only proxy scores. The reference model remains frozen and evaluates the resulting answers. The authors compare two methods: generate several candidate answers and select the highest proxy score, or update the policy with reinforcement learning so that high-scoring outputs become more likely. They also vary reward-model size and training-data volume, tracking both scores as optimization increases. The proxy score can keep rising while the reference score first rises and then falls. Better proxy models generally delay or reduce this divergence. The proxy guides improvement over a range, while continued optimization exposes its errors.  How optimization amplifies evaluation errors Limited-discriminator theory studies which differences an evaluator cannot detect. Reward overoptimization asks how those omissions and errors shape outputs when an optimizer specifically pursues the evaluator's highest scores. In Berk–Nash, evidence selected by actions feeds back into beliefs. Reward overoptimization can occur with a completely frozen evaluator. For RSI, this motivates checking whether a modification concentrates behavior on outputs the current evaluator overvalues, and evaluating those outputs against a fixed reference standard.  Source and reading guide Gao, Schulman, and Hilton, 2023,  Scaling Laws for Reward Model Overoptimization , ICML. Paper page and published PDF. See §2.1 for models and data; §§3.1–3.6 for the two optimization methods, empirical curves, and model-size comparisons; §4.5 for further discussion.", "paper": "Gao, Schulman & Hilton (2023). Scaling Laws for Reward Model Overoptimization. ICML."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "limited-discriminators", "title": "Finite discriminators: indistinguishable distributions need not be identical", "category": "Feedback and learning dynamics", "text": "Finite discriminators: indistinguishable distributions need not be identical A generative adversarial network trains a generator to produce samples and a discriminator to distinguish them from real data. Arora and colleagues ask: when the discriminator cannot tell them apart, has the generator learned the real distribution, or merely satisfied the checks available to that discriminator?  Discriminative capacity determines the measure of closeness Let P be the real distribution, Q the generated distribution, and 𝓕 the allowed class of discriminator functions. Each function f assigns a score to a sample. The following distance takes the largest difference in average scores that an allowed function can detect:   The expectations are over real and generated samples respectively. A small distance means this class of discriminators has difficulty separating the distributions. Differences in structures the discriminators cannot test may go undetected.  Generalization can coexist with missing diversity A failure to distinguish training samples might simply reflect too little data. Under conditions including a bounded measuring function and smooth dependence on network parameters, the paper shows that finite samples can estimate the corresponding neural-network distance. Closeness as judged by the restricted discriminators can therefore generalize from samples to population distributions. The authors also construct distributions with very small support that are difficult for finite discriminators to distinguish from the real distribution, despite missing much of its diversity. More data can improve estimation of the restricted distance; the discriminator class still determines what is being tested. The paper also experiments with mixtures of generators and discriminators on images, comparing sample quality and generation metrics. The theoretical construction explains why finite discriminative capacity permits omissions; the experiments examine the mixture-based training method.  Reading Berk–Nash and model collapse through feedback Comparing the feedback processes, finite-discriminator theory studies what can be recognized after samples arrive. Berk–Nash studies how actions select evidence and subjective models interpret it. Model collapse studies how omissions and errors propagate when a previous model becomes the data source. Reward overoptimization further examines how pursuing the current score changes outputs. For RSI, evaluators' discriminative capacity affects which improvements can be recognized and retained. How feedback corrects a learner connects these steps and uses board games and code to illustrate effective feedback.  Source and reading guide Arora and colleagues, 2017,  Generalization and Equilibrium in Generative Adversarial Nets (GANs) , ICML. Paper page and published PDF. Generalization: §3.3, Theorem 3.1. Small-support construction: §3.4, Corollary 3.1. Image experiments: §6. The displayed formula uses a linear measuring function to explain the restricted distance; the paper allows more general measuring functions.", "paper": "Arora et al. (2017). Generalization and Equilibrium in Generative Adversarial Nets (GANs). ICML."}, {"lang": "en", "slug": "feedback", "title": "How feedback corrects a learner", "category": "Feedback and learning dynamics", "text": "How feedback corrects a learner Good feedback turns one attempt into evidence for improving the next. Berk–Nash equilibrium, GANs and model collapse explain different parts of this process:  actions determine which evidence is observed, evaluators determine which differences are recognized, and data sources determine what reaches the next generation.  Board games and code show how clear, executable feedback can support learning.  Berk–Nash Actions determine which beliefs are tested In a Berk–Nash equilibrium, beliefs support only the models that best fit the data induced by current actions, while those actions are optimal under the beliefs. Actions select evidence; evidence supports an explanation; that explanation supports the actions. Even when responses come from the real world, errors about untried actions can persist. If the subjective model class is misspecified, it may also fail to explain observed responses correctly. Exploration expands the range of tests, while model revision expands the explanations available. Together, they determine what real feedback can correct.  GANs Evaluators determine which differences guide learning A GAN discriminator sees both real and generated samples. It learns to distinguish their sources and turns the differences into a training signal for the generator. Real samples provide an ongoing reference; the discriminator makes that reference usable. Arora and colleagues' results on limited discriminators show that a generated distribution can lack substantial diversity yet remain difficult for a restricted discriminator to distinguish. Alongside Berk–Nash's analysis of evidence selection, this raises another question: once evidence is available, can the evaluator recognize the differences that matter? More samples and greater discriminating ability improve different parts of the process.  Model collapse Data sources determine how errors are inherited Model collapse concerns successive models learning from samples generated by their predecessors. Finite sampling can omit rare events, leading later models to assign them still less probability. Fitting errors also travel through the changing data source. Compared with continued access to world responses or real samples, replacing data each generation changes the basis for correction itself. Research on data accumulation changes this error transmission by retaining original and historical data. These three strands let us examine feedback coverage, discriminating ability and provenance together.  Board games Rules and search provide training targets Board games such as chess, shogi, and Go offer strong feedback: rules are explicit, state transitions can be computed, and final outcomes can be checked. AlphaZero uses its current network to estimate moves and position values, then uses tree search to choose actions. After each game, the network is trained to predict the rule-determined outcome and the move distribution produced by search. The updated network returns to self-play. The system generates its own games, but the rules determine the results. Search combines multi-step simulations with network estimates; training turns some of that search experience into cheaper future judgments. Self-play can therefore generate repeated experience, allowing reliable outcome feedback and additional computation to support learning together. Knowing the rules does not mean having computed all their consequences. Progress in board games can come from computing, accumulating and reusing those consequences, illustrating how computation makes existing knowledge more useful.  Code Execution makes candidates testable Code offers another strong feedback setting. Candidate programs can be compiled and tested, producing explicit outcomes such as compilation errors, runtime errors, failed tests and passed tests. CodeRL trains a critic on these outcomes and uses it to turn execution feedback into signals for training the generator. During generation, CodeRL also uses example tests to filter candidates, selects prefixes with the critic, and generates again. Its experiments compare training signals and the regeneration step separately: the training ablations support using critic-derived weights for individual code tokens, while the regeneration comparison shows that candidate sets more often contain a program that passes the tests. This separates two sources of improvement: learning from execution feedback, and using checks to revise candidates during generation. Compilation and testing provide explicit, repeatable checks for specified requirements. The model proposes a program, execution produces a result, and learning uses successes and failures to guide later generation. This adds an executable route for correction beyond asking the model to score its own code.  Connecting feedback to recursive improvement Board games and code make the productive role of feedback clear: outcomes have explicit grounds, attempts can be repeated, and experience can improve subsequent choices. Berk–Nash, GANs and model collapse help distinguish the parts of this process. RSI asks whether a system can also improve how it obtains, interprets and uses feedback. Plasticity research concerns the capacity to keep learning from experience. STOP and HyperAgents bring the programs that produce modifications into the learning process. Feedback can thus evaluate current behavior and guide improvements to the mechanisms of future learning.  Sources and reading locations - The linked explainers provide the original sources, versions and conditions for Berk–Nash, limited GAN discriminators, model collapse and data accumulation. - Silver et al. (2018),  A general reinforcement learning algorithm that masters chess, shogi, and Go through self-play , Science. Publication; author manuscript and supplement. Main text pp. 2–3 and equation (1) describe the training loop; the supplement's Domain Knowledge, Search and Configuration sections describe rules and exploration; Figures 1–3 report playing evaluations. - Le et al. (2022),  CodeRL: Mastering Code Generation through Pretrained Models and Deep Reinforcement Learning , NeurIPS. Conference paper; supplement. Section 3.3 and Figure 2 describe execution feedback and training; Section 4.1 gives the training sequence. Figure 3 and supplementary Algorithm 1 describe filtering and regeneration; Tables 2 and 4 compare training signals and generation steps.", "paper": ""}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "internal-rewards", "title": "内部奖励为什么可以不同于最终目标", "category": "价值与评价", "text": "内部奖励为什么可以不同于最终目标 如果最终任务是找食物，却要先学会开箱，训练时应该只奖励吃到食物，还是也奖励开箱？Singh 等说明：帮助有限学习者掌握任务的信号，可以不同于最终评价它的标准。  训练奖励服务于什么目标 论文的一组网格世界实验在早期不提供食物。只按食物收益训练，学习者几乎得不到行动指引；若内部奖励鼓励开箱，提前学到的行为便能帮助后来的取食。内部奖励指导眼前的学习，外部适应度则评价整个生命周期的收益。 用 r 表示内部奖励，A 表示给定学习算法，A(r) 是按它学出的策略。若 J 评价终生表现，奖励设计就是寻找使 J(A(r)) 更高的 r。同一种奖励对不同能力、记忆或学习速度的主体，效果可能不同；奖励的价值取决于它怎样帮助这个学习者。  实验怎样比较奖励设计 作者在给定奖励空间中搜索内部奖励，与直接使用外部适应度训练的学习者比较。提前学习开箱有助于后续取食，说明暂时没有外部收益的行为，也可能提高终生收益。另一组部分可观测觅食实验考察了依赖学习者内部状态的奖励。 这些实验固定学习者与奖励搜索方式，比较的是不同训练信号最终实现外部目标的效果。  从内部奖励到自动改写奖励 Eureka采用这一奖励设计思路，让语言模型写出奖励、训练策略，再根据反馈修改奖励。两项工作都保留固定外部标准。效用自修改则进一步追问：评价标准本身也改变时，该依据哪个目标判断修改是否更好？  原文与阅读位置 Singh, Lewis, Barto 与 Sorg，2010， Intrinsically Motivated Reinforcement Learning: An Evolutionary Perspective 。阅读期刊版 §IV、式(1)、§V及实验图表。 期刊信息 · 作者论文 PDF", "paper": "Singh et al. (2010). Intrinsically Motivated Reinforcement Learning: An Evolutionary Perspective. IEEE TAMD."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "eureka", "title": "Eureka 如何用反馈改写训练奖励", "category": "价值与评价", "text": "Eureka 如何用反馈改写训练奖励 给机器人任务目标，还需要设计帮助它学会任务的奖励。Eureka 让语言模型反复改写奖励代码，用策略训练的结果指导下一轮设计。  训练信号会变 任务评价保持固定 奖励程序在训练中指导策略，外部任务评分评价训练后的策略。不同奖励产生的策略都用这个固定标准比较。目标是提高 F(A(R))：R 是奖励程序，A(R) 是按它训练出的策略，F 是任务评价。这延续了内部奖励与外部目标分离的思路。  反馈怎样进入下一轮修改 语言模型读取任务说明和环境代码，生成若干奖励程序。强化学习算法分别按这些奖励训练策略，记录任务表现及各奖励项的训练统计。 语言模型再结合奖励代码读取反馈。任务表现帮助它判断哪些设计值得继续；各奖励项的数值大小和变化趋势，则帮助它将训练表现与代码联系起来，决定下一轮修改哪些部分。  两项对照检验了什么 更多候选本身也可能带来好奖励。作者因此比较相同候选总数下的一次性生成与分轮生成；后者使用前轮训练反馈，获得更好的结果。这支持反馈迭代的作用，而不仅是尝试次数的增加；候选数匹配并不代表全部计算成本相同。 另一项对照保留任务得分，却移除各奖励项的细粒度反馈。效果下降说明，在所测试的任务和搜索流程中，训练统计能在任务得分之外帮助奖励设计。 循环修改的是奖励代码，基础语言模型、强化学习算法、任务评价与搜索框架保持固定。Eureka 因而连接了训练反馈与训练手段的改进；效用自修改研究的则是评价目标本身改变后的选择依据。  原文与阅读位置 Ma 等，2024， Eureka: Human-Level Reward Design via Coding Large Language Models ，ICLR；使用 arXiv v2。重点是 Definition 2.1、§3.2–3.3、§4.1–4.3及反馈消融。 原论文 · 全文", "paper": "Ma et al. (2024). Eureka: Human-Level Reward Design via Coding Large Language Models. ICLR; arXiv v2."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "utility-self-modification", "title": "改写目标时用哪个目标判断改进", "category": "价值与评价", "text": "改写目标时用哪个目标判断改进 如果系统把“完成任务才算成功”改成“所有结果都算成功”，自我评分就能提高，行为却未必改善。Everitt 等研究：主体可以修改目标时，应当用哪个目标评价这次修改？  三种评价方式 第一种按修改后的效用打分。此时，改成一个永远赋予满效用的目标，本身就可能成为最优修改。 第二种沿用当前效用，却仍按未修改的行为方式预测未来。它保留原标准，但忽略修改会怎样改变后续行动。 第三种既使用当前效用，也预测修改后的实际行为。论文称之为 realistic 评价：修改后会做什么，这些行为按现在的目标是否更好？  保持原目标最优性的条件 论文证明，在第三种规则下，若初始策略最优，且信念和初始效用满足修改独立性等条件，实际路径上的行为能够保持对原目标的最优性。 修改独立性要求：外部行动与观测历史相同时，不能仅因内部程序的改写记录不同，就改变效用评价或环境信念。修改仍可通过改变未来行动影响结果。定理保持的是行为对原目标的最优性，不要求目标代码逐字不变。  与改写训练奖励的区别 Eureka改变训练奖励，却始终用同一个任务标准评价策略，比较的是实现既定目标的不同办法。效用自修改允许这个标准也改变，因此需要把评价规则与行为预测同时写清，才能判断收益来自更好的行为，还是换了一套评分方式。  原文与阅读位置 Everitt, Filan, Daswani 与 Hutter，2016， Self-Modification of Policy and Utility Function in Rational Agents ，AGI；使用 arXiv v1。重点是 §3、Assumption 8、Definitions 9–12、Theorems 14–16。 原论文 · 全文", "paper": "Everitt et al. (2016). Self-Modification of Policy and Utility Function in Rational Agents. AGI; v1."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "metareasoning", "title": "元决策论怎样判断是否值得继续计算", "category": "价值与评价", "text": "元决策论怎样判断是否值得继续计算 下棋程序已经找到一个不错的落子，还要不要模拟其他走法？Hay 等把这个问题写成决策：计算的价值取决于它能怎样改善最后执行的行动，以及要付出多少成本。  把计算本身作为决策 模拟并不在真实棋盘上落子，而是提供结果，更新程序对候选着法的判断。论文把已完成的计算及其结果作为元层状态；主体可以继续选择计算，也可以停止并执行当前最好的行动。目标是最终行动的效用减去计算成本。 若一次模拟几乎不会改变选择，降低不确定性的任务价值也可能很小；若两个有竞争力的行动尚难区分，能改变选择的计算就更有价值。一次计算还会影响后面哪些计算值得做，所以最优策略一般需要评价后续计算序列。论文用元层马尔可夫决策过程描述这一选择。  在选择问题与围棋中分配计算 完整求解元层问题本身也很昂贵。作者发展近似方法和启发式，在候选选择问题与围棋中比较模拟资源的分配。围棋提供了清楚的反馈场景：模拟按已知规则展开，结果可以比较候选着法；问题在于如何用有限模拟次数作出更好的选择。 这里付出的是内部计算成本。它与真实尝试一个行动而损失的收益不同，因此搜索与验证可以在尚未执行外部行动时改善决策。  从计算选择到程序自修改 Gödel machine把选择进一步扩展为：现在修改程序，还是继续搜索？它允许负责搜索证明的程序也被改写。两项工作都把“继续计算是否值得”纳入效用评价，后者还改变以后计算的方式。  原文与阅读位置 Hay 等，2012， Selecting Computations: Theory and Applications ，UAI。重点是 §2 的定义、§4–6 的分析与应用。 正式出处 · 所读全文", "paper": "Hay et al. (2012). Selecting Computations: Theory and Applications. UAI."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "godel-machine", "title": "Gödel machine 怎样接受一次自修改", "category": "价值与评价", "text": "Gödel machine 怎样接受一次自修改 找到一个可能有用的修改后，程序应该立即采用，还是继续搜索更好的方案？Gödel machine 用证明条件决定何时停止搜索并修改自身。  自修改的接受条件 系统用公理描述自身运行、环境及效用目标。证明搜索程序寻找候选修改，并尝试证明：立即执行修改的预期未来效用，高于继续运行当前搜索过程。 找到证明后，系统执行修改。可替换的部分包括搜索修改和证明的程序，因此一次修改能够改变以后怎样提出、检查和实施下一次修改。  为什么比较对象是继续搜索 继续搜索包含以后发现其他方案的可能性。只把候选修改与“永远保持当前程序不变”比较，无法回答为什么不再找一会儿。这里的接受条件把等待、搜索及未来自修改都计入判断，使停止搜索与接受修改成为同一个效用问题。 最优性结果相对于系统的公理成立。实际有效的修改可能无法在其中证明，存在的证明也可能未被及时找到；因此结果约束的是获得证明后的修改选择。  证明接受与实验接受 元决策论同样把计算成本与最终选择联系起来；Gödel machine 还允许改变计算程序自身。现代奖励设计系统 Eureka则训练使用候选奖励的策略，以任务表现决定怎样继续修改。前者以证明接受修改，后者以训练实验评价候选，分别提供了自修改的形式化标准与可运行的反馈过程。  原文与阅读位置 Schmidhuber，初稿2003，使用2006年的 v5， Gödel Machines: Self-Referential Universal Problem Solvers Making Provably Optimal Self-Improvements 。重点是 §2.4、Theorem 4.1与 §4.3。 原论文 · 全文", "paper": "Schmidhuber (2003/2006). Gödel Machines: Self-Referential Universal Problem Solvers Making Provably Optimal Self-Improvements. v5."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "blackwell-information", "title": "Blackwell 信息怎样改善决策", "category": "认识与交互", "text": "Blackwell 信息怎样改善决策 假设你要根据温度决定是否开机。一个仪表显示具体温度，另一个只显示“高于或低于阈值”。只看高低就能完成的决策，拿到具体温度的人也能完成：先把数值转成高低，再照原来的办法选择。这个示意抓住了 Blackwell 信息比较的出发点：能否用一种信号重现另一种信号所支持的决策。  详细信号可以怎样模拟较粗的信号 用 w 表示未知的真实状态。渠道 A 给出信号 x，渠道 B 给出信号 z。设存在一个固定的加工规则 K，只根据 x 删减信息或加入随机性，就能产生与 B 相同的信号分布：   两个 Q 分别描述真实状态为 w 时，各渠道会给出什么信号。K(z x) 表示收到 x 后，把它加工成 z 的概率。关键条件是 K 只能使用收到的信号，不能偷偷读取未知状态 w；否则，额外信息可能来自加工过程。 现在考虑任何一个根据 B 选择行动的办法。拿到 A 的决策者先按 K 生成 z，再按那个办法行动，就能重现使用 B 时的效果。因此，A 至少保留了 B 所支持的全部决策办法。在先验、收益函数相同，不计获取成本，并允许最优使用信息时，A 的最优期望收益不会更低。  更多信息何时才变成更好结果 这个结论是“不更差”，不要求结果严格变好。开机只需要知道是否越过阈值时，具体温度可能没有额外价值。只有额外细节能够改变值得采取的行动，它们才可能改善结果。信息的决策价值因此取决于任务，而不只是信号有多长或多复杂。 上述论证允许决策者自由模拟旧渠道并选择最佳策略。实际学习还要考虑信息的获取成本与使用难度：可用信息研究有限预测模型能利用多少证据，双重控制研究行动怎样决定以后拿到的证据。  原论文与核验范围 David Blackwell（1953）， Equivalent Comparisons of Experiments ，Annals of Mathematical Statistics 24：265–272。正式出处；作者学术资料与摘要入口。 核验限于书目与摘要。本页仅说明“信号加工推出决策价值不降”的方向，未核验原文的完整等价定理。", "paper": "Blackwell (1953). Equivalent Comparisons of Experiments. Ann. Math. Stat. 24:265–272. †"}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "dual-control", "title": "双重控制 行动也在为后续学习获取证据", "category": "认识与交互", "text": "双重控制 行动也在为后续学习获取证据 以推动一个阻力未知的物体为例：力度影响它移动多远，观察位移又能帮助判断阻力。一次动作同时改变任务状态和对环境的认识。双重控制研究：眼前最省力的动作，是否会让我们错过对以后有用的试探？  为什么把未知参数换成估计还不够 Klenske 与 Hennig 研究未知动力学下、持续一段有限时间的控制问题。决策者一面跟踪系统当前状态，一面用概率分布记录对未知参数的判断。动作执行后，物理状态改变；新观测也会调整参数的概率。下一步选择必须面对这两种变化。 一种常见简化是先估计参数，再把估计当作事实来控制系统。这叫确定性等价控制。它利用了已经学到的知识，却不计算当前动作能给未来学习带来什么帮助。双重控制把这一帮助也算进去：一个动作即使眼前代价稍高，只要它能消除关键疑问、改善之后的动作，就可能值得做。 这样规划的困难在于，尚未执行动作时并不知道会收到哪条反馈。每个动作都对应多种可能观测，每种观测又产生不同的参数信念和后续决策。论文围绕一条预先计算的参考轨迹作局部二阶近似，以近似未来代价，得到能够实际计算的控制器。  实验怎样判断探索是否用在了合适的地方 作者比较四类控制器：直接知道真实动力学的控制器、确定性等价控制器、额外奖励探索的控制器，以及近似双重控制器。模拟任务改变了探索的时机与成本、未知参数对后续任务的重要性，以及知识会多快失效。 若稍后试探更便宜，而且等待期间缺少这些知识的代价较小，就可以推迟探索；若某个参数不影响后续动作，就不值得付出很多试探成本；若知识很快失效，提前学习的价值也会降低。模拟展示了近似双重控制如何据此选择探索。  从信息价值到学习中的行动选择 Blackwell 信息比较比较给定信号能够支持的决策。双重控制把信号获取也交给行动：当前理解决定怎么试，试出来的结果又改变以后的理解。信息导向采样进一步把探索集中到“哪个行动最好”这个未知对象。 动力学模型的形式和近似规划方法仍固定，变化的是信念及据此选择的动作。  原论文与核验位置 Edgar Klenske、Philipp Hennig（2016）， Dual Control for Approximate Bayesian Reinforcement Learning ，JMLR 17（127）。论文主页；全文 PDF。 核验位置：§3.1、式（7）、§4 与 §6.1–6.5；实验对照见图1、5–7及表1。", "paper": "Klenske & Hennig (2016). Dual Control for Approximate Bayesian Reinforcement Learning. JMLR 17(127)."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "information-directed-sampling", "title": "信息导向采样 探索应服务于找到最优行动", "category": "认识与交互", "text": "信息导向采样 探索应服务于找到最优行动 一个学习者面对多个选项时，可以试探许多尚不清楚的事情，但只有部分未知会改变哪个选项最好。Russo 与 Van Roy 的信息导向采样把探索目标限定在这里：付出多少眼前收益，能够换来多少关于最优行动的信息？  每次试探同时有损失和收获 设 A★ 是未知环境中收益最高的行动。因为环境未知，学习者只能根据已有记录，判断各行动有多大概率成为 A★；这就是关于最优行动的后验分布。 对一个候选行动 a，Δₜ(a) 是选择 a 相对选择 A★ 的预期收益损失，称为预期遗憾；gₜ(a) 是观察该行动的反馈后，预计减少的关于 A★ 的不确定性，用互信息衡量。两者都依据第 t 步以前的记录计算。 算法还可以按概率随机选择行动。若 p(a) 表示选择 a 的概率，论文最小化下面的信息比：   分子是预期损失的平方，分母是预期信息收获。优化的是整组选择概率 p；随机混合行动可能改善这个权衡，因而不等于分别给每个行动打分后取最低者。  为什么不去了解所有未知 考虑一种示意情形：某个环境参数仍然很不确定，但无论它取什么值，同一个行动都最好。进一步了解该参数可以增加世界知识，却没有帮助辨认 A★。相反，一条简单反馈只要能排除当前最有竞争力的错误选项，就可能具有较高的信息价值。 论文采用给定的贝叶斯环境模型和有限行动集。把反馈换算成收益的函数已知，收益有界；未知的是环境决定的反馈分布。给定环境后，各轮潜在反馈独立同分布，但学习者实际看到哪些反馈仍由行动决定。 理论把整段交互的总遗憾，与信息比和最优行动的初始不确定性联系起来：信息比若有统一上界，就能控制累计损失。伯努利与线性老虎机实验再与上置信界、Thompson sampling、知识梯度等方法比较，评价的是一段交互累计失去多少收益。  它解决哪一段反馈循环 双重控制联合考虑当前行动与未来学习；信息导向采样围绕最优行动分配探索。两者都在给定环境模型中推断。选到有区分力的反馈之后，学习器还要能读出其中的区别；可用信息研究后一步。  原论文与核验位置 Daniel Russo、Benjamin Van Roy（2014）， Learning to Optimize via Information-Directed Sampling ，NeurIPS。正式全文 PDF。 核验位置：§2–4、式（5）–（6）、Proposition 2 与 §6。公式以行动分布写出信息比。", "paper": "Russo & Van Roy (2014). Learning to Optimize via Information-Directed Sampling. NeurIPS."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "usable-information", "title": "可用信息 同一份证据为何对不同学习者作用不同", "category": "认识与交互", "text": "可用信息 同一份证据为何对不同学习者作用不同 一份数据包含某个规律，不代表任何模型都能读出它。一个简单示意是：两个输入各自独立、等概率取 +1 或 −1，结果由它们的乘积决定，而模型只能对给定特征作线性读出。直接给两个输入与先算出乘积再交给模型，使用的是同一份证据，却可能产生完全不同的预测效果。Xu 等用“可用信息”描述这种差别。  先固定谁在使用信息 设 X 是输入，Y 是要预测的结果，𝒱 是允许使用的预测模型集合。模型 f 看到 X 后，为 Y 的各个可能取值分配概率。f(X)(Y) 表示它给实际出现的结果所分配的概率。 论文用对数损失评价预测。模型如果反复给实际发生的结果很低概率，就会付出较大的损失。比较集合中所有允许的模型，取它们能够达到的最低平均损失，得到条件 V-熵：   这里的 inf 表示这组模型损失能够逼近的最低值，期望是对数据平均。再计算不看 X 时同类模型能达到的最低损失 H𝒱(Y)，两者的差就是输入提供的可用信息：   这个差回答的是：允许这类模型看输入，最多能把预测改善多少？若模型族允许忽略输入，多给输入至少可以保持原来的最佳预测办法，所以差值非负。  计算为什么能增加可用性 仅对输入作确定性处理，不会增加它与预测目标之间的 Shannon 互信息。可是，前面的乘积示意说明，处理可以把同一规律改写成某类模型容易使用的形式。这里没有引入新证据，改变的是证据对当前预测者是否有用。 论文在合成树结构恢复等任务中比较不同信息估计方法。任务要求根据数据判断变量之间的联系，因此，估计器如何衡量关系中的信息，会影响最终恢复的结构。  可用多少与已经学到多少要分开 定义取的是模型族中的最佳预测表现。某次训练可能尚未找到这个模型，所以可用信息不等于当前模型已经利用的信息。当前损失离类内最优多远，是训练进度问题；换一个更强模型族后，最优值还能下降多少，是能力范围问题。 合并阶梯研究训练能沿什么路径获得新特征，压缩进展比较一次更新前后的实际改善。它们分别研究可用信息如何被学到，以及学习已经带来多少变化。  原论文与核验位置 Yilun Xu 等（2020）， A Theory of Usable Information under Computational Constraints ，ICLR。所用 arXiv v1；正文。 核验位置：定义1–3、命题2、§3.2、定理1及 §6.1。", "paper": "Xu et al. (2020). A Theory of Usable Information under Computational Constraints. ICLR; v1."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "merged-staircase", "title": "合并阶梯 简单特征怎样成为后续学习的起点", "category": "认识与交互", "text": "合并阶梯 简单特征怎样成为后续学习的起点 如果一个目标只在三个变量同时出现时才表现出规律，逐个查看变量可能什么也看不出来。若目标还包含单变量与两变量的规律，训练就多了逐步进入复杂结构的机会。Abbe 等研究的正是这种学习路径，而不只是网络能否表示目标函数。  一个目标提供起点 另一个没有 论文比较下面两种函数，其中每个输入变量独立地以相同概率取 +1 或 −1：   第一种目标包含 x₁。单看 x₁，就能发现它与输出有关；已经学到的特征又可能帮助训练识别后面的乘积项。第二种目标只有三变量乘积，单个变量或两变量乘积与输出都不相关，因而缺少同样的低阶信号。  合并阶梯把逐级学习写成条件 将目标写成若干非零乘积项。用 Sₖ 表示第 k 项涉及的变量集合。“合并阶梯”要求这些项可以排序，使每个新项至多引入一个此前尚未出现的变量：   公式中的并集包含此前所有项已经使用的变量；集合差留下新出现的变量；竖线表示变量个数。新项可以复用多个早期项中的变量，所以它比沿着一条固定的乘积链增长更一般。  成立条件怎样限制结论 作者考察只依赖少量输入坐标的稀疏目标、两层网络，以及指定尺度下的批量随机梯度训练。分析借助平均场极限，用参数分布随训练的演化近似宽网络，并研究样本量随输入维数同阶增长的情形。 在这些训练和资源条件下，可学目标必须具有合并阶梯结构。反过来，具有这一结构的目标对几乎所有系数组合都可学，但某些特殊组合不在保证之内。 实验跟踪不同项被学到的先后，也展示特殊系数组合造成的退化。这里的学习困难由指定算法与资源共同决定。  这对理解能力增长有什么帮助 可用信息衡量一类模型能达到的最佳预测；合并阶梯进一步问，训练是否有路走到那里。在固定架构与更新算法下，已有特征也可以改变后续学习的起点。  原论文与核验位置 Emmanuel Abbe、Enric Boix-Adserà、Theodor Misiakiewicz（2022）， The merged-staircase property: a necessary and nearly sufficient condition for SGD learning of sparse functions on two-layer neural networks ，COLT。论文主页；全文 PDF。 核验位置：第3页动机例子、§1.1、§4及图2。", "paper": "Abbe et al. (2022). The merged-staircase property: a necessary and nearly sufficient condition for SGD learning of sparse functions on two-layer neural networks. COLT."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "compression-progress", "title": "压缩进展 用刚学会的规律选择下一段经验", "category": "认识与交互", "text": "压缩进展 用刚学会的规律选择下一段经验 反复观察已经掌握的规律，通常学不到多少新东西；观看纯噪声，又可能永远找不到规律。学习者应在两者之间寻找什么？Schmidhuber 的回答是：寻找能让自己取得学习进展的经验，并用压缩改善来衡量这种进展。  压缩改善怎样表示学会了规律 一个压缩器如果找到了数据中的规律，就可以用“规律加例外”的方式描述它，少记一些逐项内容。因此，可以比较同一段历史在学习前后需要多少编码。 设 h 是固定的一份历史，C旧 与 C新 分别是更新前后的压缩器，L(C,h) 是压缩器 C 描述 h 所需的编码代价。进展奖励可写为：   差值为正，表示新压缩器用更少代价描述了同一批经历。比较必须使用同一份历史：若旧压缩器处理难数据、新压缩器处理容易数据，代价下降就未必来自学习。  把学习进展变成下一次行动的理由 系统把这个奖励交给选择行动的控制器。控制器尝试选择可能带来压缩改善的经验；压缩器吸收经验后，实际改善又成为控制器的反馈。于是，学习结果会影响以后去哪里、观察什么，而后续观察继续改变学习结果。 已经熟悉的数据虽然容易压缩，却可能不再带来进展。纯噪声虽然不断变化，也通常无法提供稳定的可复用规律。进展奖励因此希望把行动引向当前尚未掌握、但有希望学会的内容。 进展相对于当前学习器：压缩器训练失败或表达能力不足时，有结构的数据也可能没有进展信号。奖励测量的是观察到的前后变化。  它与其他学习量有什么区别 可用信息问观察输入能使某类模型的预测改善多少；epiplexity刻画计算受限时描述数据所需的结构；压缩进展比较新旧压缩器在同一历史上的表现，把已经发生的学习转成未来经验选择的依据。 本文提出框架、形式化并回顾已有实现。上述循环可以使用固定的压缩器更新规则。  原论文与核验位置 Jürgen Schmidhuber（2008初稿，2009修订）， Driven by Compression Progress: A Simple Principle Explains Essential Aspects of Subjective Beauty, Novelty, Surprise, Interestingness, Attention, Curiosity, Creativity, Art, Science, Music, Jokes 。所用 arXiv v2；正文。 核验位置：§1.2–1.3、§3、附录 A.5–A.7，尤其式（5）–（6）。", "paper": "Schmidhuber (2009). Driven by Compression Progress: A Simple Principle Explains Essential Aspects of Subjective Beauty, Novelty, Surprise, Interestingness, Attention, Curiosity, Creativity, Art, Science, Music, Jokes. arXiv v2."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "epiplexity", "title": "Epiplexity 有限计算者能够学到多少结构", "category": "认识与交互", "text": "Epiplexity 有限计算者能够学到多少结构 数据很难预测，可能有两种原因：它接近随机，或者它包含学习者还没理解的复杂规律。两者都可能产生很大的预测误差，却不提供同样的学习机会。Finzi 等提出 epiplexity，用一个受计算限制的编码问题把可描述结构与剩余不可预测内容分开。  描述数据需要写下模型 也需要写下剩余内容 先选一个概率模型 P，再用它编码数据 X。总成本有两部分：写出 P 的程序需要多少比特，以及使用 P 后仍需多少比特描述数据。论文在满足给定运行时间限制的程序中，选择总成本最小者：   这里 𝒫ₜ 表示在时间限制 T 内能够完成相应采样和概率计算的程序集合； P  是程序本身的描述长度；后面的期望是编码数据仍需的平均长度。P★ 表示取得最佳总编码的程序。Epiplexity 取其中第一部分  P★ ，配套的限时熵对应第二部分。 程序长度衡量写下模型的成本，运行时间限制决定哪些模型可以参加比较。  为什么结构复杂与纯噪声不同 对没有可利用规律的随机数据，用很复杂的程序未必能减少剩余编码，因此不值得支付额外的模型描述成本。对某些有组织的数据，更长的程序却能概括大量规律，换来更短的总描述。Epiplexity 衡量的正是这种最优描述中分配给模型的部分，而不是全部预测误差。 这个量依赖计算时间和编码方式。放宽约束后，原先只能作为剩余内容记录的部分，可能变成程序能解释的结构。  实验怎样检验结构的任务价值 作者把同一棋局信息按两种顺序组织为预训练数据：先给棋盘再给着法，或先给着法再给棋盘。前一种顺序具有更高的估计 epiplexity。后续微调与测试比较发现，两种顺序训练出的模型解棋题的表现相当，而前者更善于预测表示局势优劣的数值。 这个比较表明，相同信息的组织方式影响有限学习者能提取什么，其收益又取决于下游任务。实验中的 epiplexity 通过训练和编码近似估计。  它衡量结构 不衡量某一轮进步 压缩进展比较一次更新前后节省了多少编码；epiplexity 刻画给定约束下最佳描述中的结构部分；V-information衡量输入对某个指定预测目标能提供多少帮助。  原论文与核验位置 Marc Finzi 等（2026）， From Entropy to Epiplexity: Rethinking Information for Computationally Bounded Intelligence ，预印本。所用 arXiv v2；全文 PDF。 核验位置：§3定义7–8、定理9–10、§4、§6.1图7和附录 C.4。", "paper": "Finzi et al. (2026). From Entropy to Epiplexity: Rethinking Information for Computationally Bounded Intelligence. 预印本 v2."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "toolformer", "title": "Toolformer 如何学会调用已有工具", "category": "认识与交互", "text": "Toolformer 如何学会调用已有工具 面对一个算术问题，语言模型可以直接回答，也可以先调用计算器。Toolformer 让模型为自己生成训练样本，学习何时调用什么工具、怎样组织输入，以及怎样使用返回结果。  模型提出调用 筛选程序判断是否有用 研究者提供工具接口和少量示例。模型在普通文本中插入候选调用，系统实际执行调用，将返回结果放回文本，再检查它是否降低了后续词的预测误差。达到筛选条件的样本进入微调数据，使模型学会这些用法。 调用位置无需人工逐条标注。工具集合、初始示例和损失筛选规则由研究者给定；微调改变的是调用行为，工具实现和样本生成流程保持固定。  两组对照分别检查训练与执行 论文让一个对照模型在同一语料上训练，但不加入工具调用，以检查额外文本训练的作用；另一组对照在测试时关闭工具调用，以检查执行工具的作用。 在算术任务中，允许调用计算器的版本优于禁用调用的版本。两者保留相同的训练参数，差别在于能否实际运行计算器，因此部分收益来自执行工具。  工具可以提供事实 也可以完成计算 查询工具可以带来输入之外的事实，计算器可以求出已有算式的结果。Toolformer 学会何时把这些工作交给现有接口。Voyager 则把自己写出的行动程序保存为可调用技能：前者学习调用已有工具，后者增加可复用的工具内容。  原论文与阅读位置 Schick 等， Toolformer: Language Models Can Teach Themselves to Use Tools ，NeurIPS 2023。本文依据 arXiv v1 全文，对应方法 §2、实验 §4.1 和 Table 4。主要关注调用样本的生成、执行与过滤，以及同语料训练和禁用接口的对照。", "paper": "Schick et al. (2023). Toolformer: Language Models Can Teach Themselves to Use Tools. NeurIPS; 核对v1."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "voyager", "title": "Voyager 如何把技能积累变成后续学习的资源", "category": "认识与交互", "text": "Voyager 如何把技能积累变成后续学习的资源 一次任务成功后，智能体能否在相近任务中复用已经解决的步骤？Voyager 在 Minecraft 中把成功的行动程序保存到技能库，供后续检索、调用和组合。  一次执行怎样变成可复用的技能 系统根据环境状态和已有能力选择任务，让语言模型生成执行代码。代码运行后，环境返回状态变化和执行错误，语言模型据此修正代码；基于语言模型的自验证另行判断任务是否完成。 成功代码进入技能库。面对新任务，系统先检索相关技能，再让语言模型使用这些程序，使新代码集中在任务差异和技能组合上。基础语言模型权重保持冻结；选题、检索、写入和自验证的规则也固定，积累的是技能内容。  删除技能库检验了什么 作者删除技能库，检查仅凭当轮选题和代码生成能否维持探索。这个版本的探索更早停滞，支持保存和调用旧技能对后续探索的作用。论文还在新世界任务中测试已有技能的迁移，使评价超出原来那次成功经历。  技能与世界模型怎样帮助下一轮 Toolformer 学习何时调用给定接口，Voyager 则把自己生成的程序加入可调用集合。旧技能参与下一轮组合，使学习成果直接帮助后续行动。 Memento 3 保存的是环境规律，用来预测尚未执行的行动会产生什么后果。技能程序给出完成任务的步骤，世界模型预测行动后果；两者分别积累可复用的做法和对环境的理解。  原论文与阅读位置 Wang 等， Voyager: An Open-Ended Embodied Agent with Large Language Models ，初稿 2023，TMLR 2024。本文依据 arXiv v2 全文，对应 §2.1–2.3 的课程、技能库和反馈过程，以及 §3.2–3.4、Figures 1 和 9 的比较。", "paper": "Wang et al. (2024; 初稿2023). Voyager: An Open-Ended Embodied Agent with Large Language Models. TMLR; 核对v2."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "memento-3", "title": "Memento 3 如何用世界模型改进下一轮交互", "category": "认识与交互", "text": "Memento 3 如何用世界模型改进下一轮交互 探索陌生游戏时，系统只做过少量动作，却需要选择下一步。Memento 3 从经历中总结环境规律，预测尚未尝试的行动会产生什么后果；新执行结果再推动规则修订。  把经验写成规则和可执行模型 系统从交互记录中提取自然语言规则，组成“规则书”，再转成可执行的世界模型。模型根据状态与行动预测后果，规划过程据此选出下一步，到真实环境执行。 实际结果与预测不一致时，系统修改规则及程序。修订经过两项检查：程序有没有偏离文字规则，以及程序能否重现已有记录中的状态变化。规则书与可执行表示跨轮更新，基础语言模型、修订流程和验证办法保持固定。  规则书能否减少外部尝试 作者删除规则书形成对照，保持执行框架、验证器、基础模型和推理强度等条件一致。在所选的消融游戏中，两组都完成任务，保留规则书的版本使用更少的环境动作，支持规则书对交互效率的帮助。游戏案例进一步展示了具体规则怎样随反馈改变。  从历史一致走向新证据 两个模型可能同样解释历史，却对未尝试行动给出不同预测。执行能区分预测的行动，才有机会发现哪种解释不对；过早依赖某个模型规划，也可能避开这些尝试。 这与 模型批判 相接：用已有记录修订解释，再通过新证据检验解释。Memento 3 将前一步落实为可运行的规则修订，并让修订后的模型参与下一轮行动。  原论文与阅读位置 Zhao 等， Memento 3: Model-Based Recursive Self-Improvement through Reflective Rulebooks ，2026 年预印本。本文依据 arXiv v1 全文，对应 §2 的规则书与验证流程、§4 Tables 2–3 的游戏及规则书消融、§5 的案例，以及 §1 对历史一致性与探索盲区的讨论。", "paper": "Zhao et al. (2026). Memento 3: Model-Based Recursive Self-Improvement through Reflective Rulebooks. 预印本 v1."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "bayesian-model-criticism", "title": "模型批判为什么不能由后验更新代替", "category": "认识与交互", "text": "模型批判为什么不能由后验更新代替 如果只允许用直线解释数据，调整斜率和截距可以找到更好的直线，却不会自动得到一条曲线。这个示意说明了 Gelman 与 Shalizi 强调的区别：在现有模型里选择参数，与发现现有模型不够用，是两种不同工作。  后验更新是在既定解释之间重新分配信任 设 D 是已经观察到的数据，θ 是模型参数。贝叶斯更新把原先的参数概率与各参数解释数据的能力结合起来：   p(θ) 是观察数据前的判断；p(D θ) 衡量参数为 θ 时这批数据出现的可能性；p(θ D) 是看完数据后的判断。符号 ∝ 表示两边只差一个使总概率为一的归一化常数。 这一步始终在既定模型内比较。若模型遗漏了关键变量或关系，数据越多，后验可能越集中于其中最合适的参数，但集中本身没有检验模型集合是否足够。直线例子中，越来越确定一条斜率，并不能消除数据里仍然存在的弯曲规律。  预测检验问的是模型有没有遗漏 模型批判先问：如果当前模型成立，数据应当表现出什么特点？再比较模型预测与实际观测，寻找有规律的差别。例如，预测误差如果随输入呈现系统变化，就提示模型可能漏掉了某种关系，而不只是参数还没有估准。 发现差别后，研究者可以重新选择变量、修改函数形式，或改变数据生成假设，然后重新拟合并检验。后验计算在当前模型里学习，预测检验和模型修订则改变接下来允许怎样解释数据。  为什么这与自我改进的反馈选择有关 Memento 3 把经验写成可修改规则，呈现了类似问题：新反馈可能只要求调整已有规则，也可能迫使系统改写规则本身。 检验能够发现哪些错误，还取决于拿到了什么数据。两个模型可能对已有经历作出同样预测，却对未尝试的行动给出不同结果。若系统一直依据其中一个模型选行动，就可能始终接触不到区分两者的证据。因此，模型修订既要利用已有反例，也需要能暴露反例的行动。  原论文与阅读位置 Gelman 与 Shalizi， Philosophy and the practice of Bayesian statistics ，正式发表于 2013 年。依据 作者 arXiv v3 全文，重点为 §2、§4.1 和 §4.3。本文属于统计建模的方法论分析。", "paper": "Gelman & Shalizi (2013). Philosophy and the practice of Bayesian statistics. BJMSP 66:8–38; 核对作者v3."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "learned-optimizers", "title": "学习型优化器如何学会更新另一个模型", "category": "学习机制", "text": "学习型优化器如何学会更新另一个模型 梯度描述参数微小变化时损失怎样变化，优化器还要决定更新方向和幅度。Andrychowicz 等让另一个网络学习这个更新规则，用梯度历史指导下一步训练。  一个模型学习怎样更新另一个模型 任务模型的参数记作 θ，优化器的参数记作 φ。优化器读取任务梯度与历史状态，输出加到 θ 上的更新量。训练优化器时，让它在一组任务上执行多步更新，再根据这些步骤的任务损失调整 φ。 于是，θ 的变化让模型学会任务，φ 的变化让优化器更会训练模型。测试新任务时，φ 固定，任务参数与优化器的内部状态继续变化。外层反向传播方法、优化算法和任务分布由研究者给定。  比较更新规则及其迁移 作者在随机二次函数、图像分类与神经风格迁移等问题中训练或测试优化器，与调节过学习率的传统方法比较。同一任务下替换更新规则，用于检验学得的优化器是否有效。 论文还改变训练步数或任务模型结构，检查更新规则能否在原训练设置之外继续使用。这使评价同时覆盖训练问题上的优化效果与跨设置迁移。  三种学习机制的区别 学习型优化器学的是怎样更新模型参数；神经架构搜索学习提出哪些模型结构；元强化学习则让固定权重的网络通过内部状态适应新任务。三者分别把经验积累在更新规则、结构选择与状态变化中。  原文与阅读位置 Andrychowicz 等，2016， Learning to learn by gradient descent by gradient descent ，NeurIPS；使用 arXiv v2。重点是 §2、式(2)–(3)、§3及迁移实验。 原论文 · 全文", "paper": "Andrychowicz et al. (2016). Learning to learn by gradient descent by gradient descent. NeurIPS; v2."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "neural-architecture-search", "title": "架构搜索怎样学习提出更好的模型", "category": "学习机制", "text": "架构搜索怎样学习提出更好的模型 随机尝试许多结构，也可能找到好网络。Zoph 与 Le 训练一个架构控制器，检验已有候选的训练反馈能否帮助它提出更好的下一批网络。  控制器怎样利用候选的表现 控制器生成描述网络结构的符号，例如层的设置或循环单元的连接方式。系统据此建立候选网络，训练其权重，再用验证集评价。控制器把评价作为奖励，通过强化学习调整以后生成不同结构的概率。 每轮因此有两种学习：候选网络学会任务，控制器学习提出网络。前者更新权重，后者更新结构的提案分布。  随机搜索对照支持什么 作者在图像分类中搜索卷积结构，在语言建模中搜索循环单元。语言建模实验将控制器搜索与随机搜索比较，既看最佳候选，也看排名靠前候选的平均表现。 控制器在这些比较中优于随机搜索，支持训练反馈能够改善后续提案。多个较好候选的表现提供了超出单个最佳结果的证据；它不要求每次提出的结构都优于上一次。  结构搜索与更新规则 控制器在设计者规定的搜索空间中学习；其自身结构与更新算法保持固定。学习型优化器改变怎样训练给定模型，架构搜索改变提出什么模型。两者都把产生任务能力的一个环节，变成可以从经验中学习的机制。  原文与阅读位置 Zoph 与 Le，2017， Neural Architecture Search with Reinforcement Learning ，ICLR；使用 arXiv v2。重点是 §3.1–3.2、§4.1–4.2及随机搜索对照。 原论文 · 全文", "paper": "Zoph & Le (2017). Neural Architecture Search with Reinforcement Learning. ICLR; v2."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "meta-reinforcement-learning", "title": "权重不变时系统怎样继续学习", "category": "学习机制", "text": "权重不变时系统怎样继续学习 权重固定的网络，仍可能根据刚发生的成功或失败调整行动。Wang 等把这种快速适应放在循环网络的内部状态中：权重保存学到的适应方式，状态随当前任务经历而变化。  跨任务训练与任务内适应 训练时，网络反复经历相关任务。它在行动后接收奖励等信息，更新内部状态，再据此选择下一步行动。外层强化学习调整权重，使网络能在整段交互中取得更好的结果；状态如何响应反馈也随之被塑造。 测试新任务时，权重固定，网络继续接收反馈、更新状态、选择行动。例如，收益变化时，网络可通过近期经历调整选择。快速适应因而发生在状态变化中，不需要重新训练权重。  实验怎样检查适应方式 论文使用选项之间有关联、收益会波动以及包含两步选择的任务。在波动环境中，作者与经典探索策略、固定学习率规则比较，并通过行为分析估计网络有多重视近期反馈。 这些任务让固定动作难以持续奏效；行为分析则检查网络是否随环境波动调整适应方式。实验同时考察任务表现与利用反馈的方式。  状态学习与参数学习 测试时写入的是新经历，如何保存并使用经历已由外层训练学成。学习型优化器显式输出另一个模型的参数更新，本文则用任务网络的状态实现适应。两者都让一种学习方法成为学习成果，而外层训练过程仍由研究者规定。  原文与阅读位置 Wang 等，2016年初稿，使用2017年的 arXiv v3， Learning to reinforcement learn 。重点是 §2.3、§3.1.3–3.1.4、§3.2及行为分析。 原论文 · 全文", "paper": "Wang et al. (2016/2017). Learning to reinforcement learn. arXiv:1611.05763v3."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "stop", "title": "STOP 怎样评价产生改进的程序", "category": "学习机制", "text": "STOP 怎样评价产生改进的程序 负责改代码的程序，能否把自己改得更会改代码？STOP 区分任务程序与改进程序：前者解决问题，后者接收任务代码和评分办法，调用语言模型提出修改。改进程序的价值由修改后的任务表现决定。  用下游表现评价自修改 研究者先让同一个改进程序修改一组任务代码，计算修改后任务的平均效用。自修改时，当前改进程序把自己的源代码作为待修改对象，生成新版；新版再接受相同的下游任务评价。表现较好的版本可以继续承担自修改。 基础语言模型、任务效用和预算机制仍由研究者给定。递归发生在组织语言模型调用的代码层，评价依据来自这些代码实际完成的修改。  对照检验改进程序是否更有用 论文的一项任务是带噪奇偶学习：任务程序从标签可能出错的样本中，学习一组比特之间的奇偶关系。作者比较自修改后的改进程序、初始改进程序、单次推理和贪心迭代，并在独立测试实例上评价各方法改出的任务程序。其他任务上的实验检查修改方法能否迁移。 使用部分基础模型时，自修改提高了平均效果；使用较弱模型时，效果也可能下降。实验因而支持依赖基础模型和任务条件的递归改进。  与 DGM 和 HyperAgents 的关系 DGM 让一个程序同时承担解题和自修改，并保存历史版本继续搜索。STOP 直接以下游修改效果优化改进程序。HyperAgents 同样区分任务程序与改进程序，允许同时编辑两者；其跨任务实验把两部分代码一起迁移，评价整个系统对新任务的帮助。  原论文与阅读位置 Zelikman、Lorch、Mackey 与 Kalai， Self-Taught Optimizer (STOP): Recursively Self-Improving Code Generation ，COLM 2024。本文依据 arXiv v3 PDF，对应 §3–4、Algorithm 1 的改进程序与递归定义，以及 §5.1–5.3、Figure 4、Table 1 的任务比较和迁移实验。", "paper": "Zelikman et al. (2024). Self-Taught Optimizer (STOP): Recursively Self-Improving Code Generation. COLM; v3."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "darwin-godel-machine", "title": "DGM 如何让后代程序继续参与自修改", "category": "学习机制", "text": "DGM 如何让后代程序继续参与自修改 编程智能体修改自身后，新版本能否继续帮助它改代码？Darwin Gödel Machine（DGM）让后代程序参与自修改，并保存多个历史版本供后续搜索。  同一个程序承担解题与自修改 在任务评价中，程序修复代码仓库中的问题或完成编程任务；在自修改中，程序读取修改请求，调整自身工具和工作流程。系统从历史版本中选择出发点，执行修改，再通过编程任务评价新版本。 可继续使用的程序以代码形式保存在档案里。暂时不占优的版本仍可能包含有用工具或流程，保留它也就保留了后续修改的起点。基础语言模型、产生修改指令的外层流程和版本选择规则固定，但负责实施修改的程序可以换成先前得到的后代。  冻结修改者与取消档案的对照 第一种对照始终让最初的程序负责修改，不让后代接替。其后续收益较早趋缓，支持后代参与修改对所测编程任务的作用。 第二种对照取消历史档案，只沿最新仍可工作的版本继续。它更容易被较差修改阻断；完整系统则能回到其他分支。这项比较检验保留搜索路径的作用，与前一项检验修改者的变化相互补充。  编程能力怎样帮助自修改 DGM 利用了解题与自修改之间的技能重合：处理代码问题的能力，也可能帮助程序修改自身。HyperAgents 将更多改进流程开放为可编辑代码，并检查跨领域迁移后的效果。 DGM 用任务实验评价候选，并保留多个分支继续搜索；原 Gödel machine 要求先证明修改有益。两者分别以经验评价和形式证明作为修改依据。  原论文与阅读位置 Zhang、Hu、Lu、Lange 与 Clune， Darwin Gödel Machine: Open-Ended Evolution of Self-Improving Agents ，初稿 2025。本文依据 arXiv v3 全文，对应 §3、Algorithms 1–3 的流程，§4.1–4.4 的任务与消融，以及 Appendices E.2–E.3。", "paper": "Zhang et al. (2025/2026). Darwin Gödel Machine: Open-Ended Evolution of Self-Improving Agents. arXiv v3."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "hyperagents", "title": "HyperAgents 怎样把改进过程也纳入修改", "category": "学习机制", "text": "HyperAgents 怎样把改进过程也纳入修改 任务程序变强，负责修改它的程序未必也变强。HyperAgents 区分任务程序与改进程序，并允许同时编辑两者：既修改解题办法，也修改以后怎样寻找解题办法。  改进程序怎样参与下一轮 改进程序可以修改任务代码，也可以修改诊断错误、生成方案和组织后续修改的代码。新版本经过任务评价后继续参与搜索，其中的改进程序又能承担下一轮修改。 DGM 已允许后代参与修改，产生修改指令的外层流程仍固定。HyperAgents 将更多这类流程开放为可编辑代码，基础语言模型、外部任务评分和主实验中的版本选择规则保持固定。  怎样单独测试改进能力 测试时固定被测改进程序，让它修改给定任务程序及已经产生的候选；随后用验证集选择版本，在测试集上比较所选版本与起始程序的表现。这检验一个确定版本能带来多少改善。候选数量受到限制，各次尝试的计算成本仍可能不同。 论文另通过冻结改进程序、取消历史档案和与 DGM 比较，考察自修改与保留分支的作用。  已有修改经验能否迁移 作者把论文评审和机器人奖励设计中得到的系统迁移到数学解答评分。在相同评估规则和候选数量限制下，迁移版本比从最初任务程序与改进程序出发的系统取得更大改善；测试期间改进程序保持固定。 两部分代码一起迁移，结果支持整个系统对新任务的帮助。改善也包括修复输出格式，使程序正常完成评分流程，因此不能全部归给改进程序或数学理解。 STOP 直接以下游修改效果优化改进程序；HyperAgents 将任务代码与改进代码共同纳入搜索，并评价共同迁移后的效果。  原论文与阅读位置 Zhang 等， Hyperagents ，2026 年预印本。本文依据 arXiv v1 全文，对应 §3–5，特别是 §5.2 的改进能力评估；Appendices C.4、D.3–D.4、E.3、E.5 补充评分、候选选择和系统细节。", "paper": "Zhang et al. (2026). Hyperagents. arXiv:2603.19461v1，预印本."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "plasticity", "title": "持续学习为什么还需要保持可塑性", "category": "学习机制", "text": "持续学习为什么还需要保持可塑性 网络训练得越久，是否仍然容易学会新任务？Dohare 等发现，积累训练经历后，网络可能越来越难适应后来的问题。继续从新经验中学习的能力，称为“可塑性”。  学不会新任务与忘记旧任务 遗忘研究检查学习新任务后还能保留多少旧知识；可塑性研究检查网络还能多好地学习新任务。论文让网络连续面对变化的任务，分别训练并检查表现。即使不要求保留旧任务，后来的任务也可能越学越差。  重置低贡献单元能否保持学习能力 作者在连续图像任务等场景中，比较常规反向传播、正则化或扰动方法，以及持续反向传播。后者在梯度更新之外检查单元贡献，重置已经训练一段时间、贡献仍低的单元，为后续学习提供重新调整的机会。 各组面对相同的任务序列。在所测序列中，加入单元重置能缓解后续表现下降，说明下降也与网络此前训练所产生的变化有关，改变更新方法可以帮助保持学习能力。单元选择与重置规则由研究者设计，在训练中保持固定。  反馈到达之后还需要学得动 系统得到新反馈后，更新过程仍可能越来越难把它转成能力。可塑性研究处理这一层问题，使它与缺少纠错证据的情况区分开。 学习型优化器让另一个模型学习参数更新规则，元强化学习通过跨任务训练塑造内部状态的适应方式。这些研究分别改变怎样更新和怎样适应；保持可塑性则使后续学习机会仍能被利用。  原文与阅读位置 期刊论文为 Dohare 等，2024， Loss of plasticity in deep continual learning ，Nature。这里的方法说明依据作者预印本 v3，题为  Maintaining Plasticity in Deep Continual Learning ；所读版本与期刊版标题和部分编排不同。重点是预印本 §2、§5、Algorithm 1及连续任务实验。 期刊信息 · 所读作者版本 · PDF", "paper": "Dohare et al. (2024). Loss of plasticity in deep continual learning. Nature 632:768–774; 方法核对作者v3."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "self-confirming-equilibrium", "title": "自我确认均衡 为什么正确的反馈仍能留下错误判断", "category": "反馈与学习动力学", "text": "自我确认均衡 为什么正确的反馈仍能留下错误判断 真实反馈能够纠正已经暴露的错误，却未必检验我们放弃的选择。Fudenberg 与 Levine 用“自我确认均衡”描述这样的状态：主体对自己实际做的事判断正确，却可能因误解其他选择而停留在较差的行动上。  没有试过的选择 怎样一直被低估 看一个示意例子：经营者认为新产品卖不出去，于是始终只卖老产品。他准确预测了老产品的销售，却从未获得新产品的销售数据。新产品可能更有市场，但每天收到的真实反馈都无法告诉他这一点。 这个状态满足两条条件：老产品在经营者自己的信念下最有利；信念又正确解释了实际发生的销售。行为与判断一致，观察也没有迫使他改变判断。 自我确认因而可以发生在没有夸大眼前收益的情况下。错误保留在未尝试的选择上，使准确的路径内预测与次优决策同时存在。  从例子到原论文的定义 原论文研究有限扩展式博弈，用行动次序描述互动，并允许参与者只能观察部分历史。参与者的计划决定他实际遇到哪些局面。定义要求：被采用的策略对某个信念最优，且信念在实际到达的信息集上正确。信息集是一组参与者凭当前观察无法进一步区分的决策局面；未到达的信息集没有受到同样的检验。 不同的正概率纯策略可以由不同信念支持。这个定义刻画行为与所见反馈怎样相互维持；学习是否收敛到这样的状态，是另一个需要分析的问题。  错误怎样避开检验 信念使主体避开某些行动，避开的行动又不产生反证。纠错因此取决于哪些反馈有机会出现。 Berk–Nash 均衡进一步处理模型错设：主体的全部模型都可能漏掉某种规律，以至于已经观察到的数据也只能被近似解释。对 RSI，增加探索改变证据范围，扩展模型类改变能够提出的解释。  原文与阅读位置 Fudenberg 与 Levine，1993， Self-Confirming Equilibrium ，Econometrica 61，523–545。正式论文 PDF。 定义见 §3、Definition 1，刊页527；动机与讨论见刊页523、528。", "paper": "Fudenberg & Levine (1993). Self-Confirming Equilibrium. Econometrica 61:523–545."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "berk-nash", "title": "Berk–Nash 均衡如何解释反馈中的错误自洽", "category": "反馈与学习动力学", "text": "Berk–Nash 均衡如何解释反馈中的错误自洽 学习者只能在现有模型中寻找最符合经验的解释，而它选择的行动又决定了会积累什么经验。Esponda 与 Pouzo 的 Berk–Nash 均衡把两步接起来：信念最符合收到的反馈，行动又对这个信念最优。两者可以相互支持，却一起偏离真实世界。  一个两行动例子 以下是说明定义的示意。学习者可以选 A 或 B，成功得到收益 1，失败得到 0。真实成功率为 A：0.6，B：0.8，所以实际更好的选择是 B。 学习者只考虑一类模型：A 的成功率是 θ，B 的成功率总是 θ 的一半。θ 可以取 0.4、0.6、0.8。这个模型类始终认定 B 比 A 差，无法表达真实世界。 假如一直选择 A，长期反馈会支持 θ＝0.6。这个参数准确解释了 A，却把 B 的成功率预测成 0.3。学习者于是继续选择 A；继续选择 A，又继续提供支持 θ＝0.6 的数据。 尝试 B 会暴露错误。但保留“B 的成功率是 A 的一半”这个限制，就找不到同时正确解释两个行动的参数。探索改变证据，修正模型类改变解释证据的可能方式。  对经验的最佳拟合 先考虑单个学习者，行动和反馈都只有有限种，收益函数保持固定。用 a 表示行动，y 表示反馈，u(a,y) 表示收益。真实世界按 Q W(y∣a) 产生反馈，学习者从参数集合 Θ 中选择模型 Q θ(y∣a)。ρ(a) 是行动 a 被选择的频率。 经常选择的行动，在评价拟合程度时占更大权重。加权 KL 散度 K 衡量预测误差，Θ (ρ) 收集误差最小的参数：   KL 表示主观模型相比真实分布多付出的平均对数损失。没有选择过的行动权重为零，数据不会直接检验它；模型的结构假设仍可能约束它。上例中，B 的预测始终与 A 共享参数。 模型类固定、行动频率稳定并满足相应正则条件时，贝叶斯更新会把后验概率集中到最佳拟合模型上：长期平均对数似然的差异，对应这里的加权 KL 差异。  最佳拟合还要支持实际行动 学习者可以同时相信多个模型。b 是参数 θ 上的信念分布，对反馈的预测是各模型预测的加权平均：   单主体 Berk–Nash 均衡要求策略 ρ 与信念 b 满足两条条件。 第一，信念概率全部落在当前行动数据下的最佳拟合模型上：   第二，实际采用的每个行动，在这个混合信念下都使预期收益最大：   第一条把反馈接到信念，第二条把信念接回行动。上例中，b 集中在 θ＝0.6，A 又是该信念下的最优行动，两条条件同时成立。原论文还允许多人和私人信号；每人的反馈分布也受他人策略影响。  与自我确认、Nash 和学习收敛的关系 自我确认均衡允许路径外的错误：对实际观察到的反馈判断正确，却误判未尝试的行动。Berk–Nash 还允许路径内的误差，因为真分布可能不在模型类中。 模型设定正确时，Berk–Nash 与自我确认均衡相联系。再加上强识别条件，使最佳拟合模型对所有行动给出相同预测，便能恢复相应的 Nash 结论。这排除了“所见数据无法区分、对未试行动却意见不同”的模型。 均衡刻画相互支持的信念与行动；学习收敛研究能否到达它们。原论文固定主观模型类，采用贝叶斯更新，并分析收益受到小扰动的博弈。Theorem 2 证明，相应最优政策下能够稳定下来的策略，必须满足该扰动博弈的 Berk–Nash 条件。 反向结论依赖额外条件。2019年勘误要求均衡处的弱识别、支持均衡的信念，以及在最佳拟合集合上具有指定条件分布的先验；政策允许优化误差逐渐消失。内生错设学习进一步分析哪些均衡能成为行动的长期极限。  与 GAN 和模型崩溃一起看反馈 从反馈过程比较，Berk–Nash 研究行动怎样选择证据、错设模型怎样解释证据；有限判别器的 GAN 理论研究样本已经提供后，评价器能分辨哪些差异；模型崩溃研究上一代输出成为下一代数据时，误差怎样传递。 RSI 可以通过探索、工具和模型修改改变这些环节。主题解读反馈怎样纠正学习者将它们连起来，并以棋类与代码说明有效反馈如何支持改进。  原文与进一步阅读 - Ignacio Esponda 与 Demian Pouzo， Berk–Nash Equilibrium: A Framework for Modeling Agents with Misspecified Models ，Econometrica 84，1093–1130，2016。正式出处。 - 使用含2019年勘误的 arXiv v4 全文。加权 KL 与均衡定义见 §2.3、Definition 1；与 Nash／自我确认的关系见 §3.2；学习结论见 §4.2、Theorem 2，§4.3及勘误。 - 自我确认均衡：没有路径外证据时，错误为何可以留下。 - 内生错设学习：哪些自洽行动可能成为学习极限。", "paper": "Esponda & Pouzo (2016). Berk–Nash Equilibrium: A Framework for Modeling Agents with Misspecified Models. Econometrica; v4含2019勘误."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "endogenous-misspecified-learning", "title": "内生错设学习 哪些自洽状态能成为长期结果", "category": "反馈与学习动力学", "text": "内生错设学习 哪些自洽状态能成为长期结果 Berk–Nash 均衡允许一种信念支持当前行动，但学习者还会继续更新信念。Fudenberg、Lanzani 与 Strack 研究：更新一直进行时，什么样的行动才能长期保持不变？  同样拟合经验的模型 可能建议不同的行动 多个模型可能具有相同的平均预测损失，却给出不同预测、推荐不同的行动。后续样本会改变学习者在这些模型之间分配的信任，行动也可能随之变化。 普通 Berk–Nash 条件要求存在一种信念：它只相信最佳拟合模型，并使当前行动最优。要说明行动在持续学习中稳定，还需检查主体转而相信另一个同样拟合得好的模型时，原行动是否仍然最优。  稳定行动必须经得起信念重新分配 记 a  为可能稳定下来的行动，Θ (a ) 为反复选择它时、最符合所得反馈的模型集合。μ 是学习者在这些模型上分配的信念概率，rθ(a) 是模型 θ 对行动 a 的预期收益。论文给出的必要条件是：   这就是 uniform Berk–Nash 条件：无论怎样在最佳拟合模型之间分配概率，原行动都最优。最佳解释之间的后验权重变化，因而不会使原行动失去最优性。 该结论针对主观模型类固定、行动和结果有限的贝叶斯决策问题：如果最优学习行为收敛，极限必须满足这个条件。论文还给出严格均衡在适当初始信念下成为长期结果的条件；一般轨迹是否收敛则需另行判断。  从当前自洽到长期稳定 均衡解释“主体目前为什么不改行动”；这篇论文进一步检查“它继续学习后，为什么仍能不改行动”。关键是最佳拟合解释之间的信任仍可能变化。 这为 RSI 提供了固定机制下的动态基线。扩展模型类、修改观测工具或改变更新方法，会改变可用解释或学习过程，原有稳定条件也需要随之重新检查。  原文与阅读位置 Fudenberg、Lanzani 与 Strack，2021， Limit Points of Endogenous Misspecified Learning ，Econometrica 89，1065–1098。正式论文 PDF。 问题、定义及 Theorems 1–2 的主要结论见导言，尤其刊页1065–1067。上式是单行动极限的说明写法。", "paper": "Fudenberg, Lanzani & Strack (2021). Limit Points of Endogenous Misspecified Learning. Econometrica 89:1065–1098."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "performative-prediction", "title": "Performative prediction 预测模型怎样改变下一轮数据", "category": "反馈与学习动力学", "text": "Performative prediction 预测模型怎样改变下一轮数据 信用评分规则部署后，申请者可能据此调整材料，下一轮训练数据也随之改变。Perdomo 等研究这种 performative prediction：模型通过部署影响环境，重训又使用环境返回的数据。  重训与部署优化的区别 用 φ 表示已部署的模型参数，D(φ) 表示它引起的数据分布。θ 是准备评价的模型参数，ℓ(z; θ) 是它在样本 z 上的损失：   第二个位置决定数据来源，第一个位置决定用哪个模型计算损失。重训收集旧模型 θₜ 诱导的数据，再选择使 R(θ; θₜ) 最小的新模型。部署优化直接最小化 R(θ; θ)：换模型时，同时考虑它会产生怎样的新数据。 如果重训停在 θ ，说明 θ  最适合 D(θ )。另一个模型却可能改变人们的行为，并在新分布上获得更低损失。因此，稳定点与部署最优点可以不同。  什么条件下重训会稳定 论文要求损失的梯度对参数和样本都平滑：改变 θ 或 z，梯度的变化幅度都由常数 β 控制。损失对参数还需具有 γ 强凸性，使当前分布下的优化具有唯一、稳定的最优解。 部署引起的数据变化用 Wasserstein-1 距离衡量，即把一个分布搬成另一个分布所需的最小平均移动距离。参数每变化一个单位，分布至多变化 ε 个单位。 当 ε < γ/β 时，总体重复风险最小化收敛到唯一稳定点：这个界给出重训稳定的充分条件。“总体”指每轮准确使用分布期望；有限样本版本另需尾部条件和随迭代增长的样本量。  反馈怎样进入优化 Outside the Echo Chamber接着研究稳定与最优的差距，通过估计环境对部署的响应来优化部署风险。Berk–Nash则研究行动选择的证据怎样支持主观信念。 Performative prediction 把模型对数据分布的影响直接写入目标。对 RSI，评价一次修改也就需要计算它对后续反馈的影响。  原文与阅读位置 Perdomo 等，2020， Performative Prediction ，ICML。论文页面及正式 PDF。 风险与稳定性的定义见 §2；收敛条件见 Theorem 3.5，相关区别见 Proposition 3.6 与 §4。", "paper": "Perdomo et al. (2020). Performative Prediction. ICML."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "outside-echo-chamber", "title": "Outside the Echo Chamber 怎样优化部署反馈后的表现", "category": "反馈与学习动力学", "text": "Outside the Echo Chamber 怎样优化部署反馈后的表现 模型在自己引起的数据上反复重训，最后可能不再变化，却仍有更好的部署方案。Miller、Perdomo 与 Zrnic 的方法是先学习环境如何响应模型，再把响应纳入优化。  为什么稳定的模型仍可能不是最优 沿用 performative prediction 的设定。θ 是部署参数，D(θ) 是部署后的数据分布，ℓ(z; θ) 是模型在样本 z 上的损失。部署风险为   改变 θ 会同时改变损失计算和数据来源。反复重训只优化前一部分：每轮固定已有的数据分布，再找最合适的参数。它停下时，当前模型对当前数据已经最好，却可能漏掉“换一个模型会让数据变得不同”带来的收益。 论文证明，稳定点的部署风险可以明显高于最优值。即使损失对参数是凸函数，整个 PR 也未必凸，因为 D(θ) 同时变化。作者因此对分布响应增加结构条件，再分析风险的性质与优化方法。  先学习响应 再优化部署 在位置族等设定下，数据变化可由分布的位置移动来描述。作者先部署模型、收集数据，估计参数如何移动分布，再利用估计出的响应优化部署风险。部署由此也为下一次决策提供了关于环境响应的信息。 实验包括带部署效应的合成线性回归，以及申请者会调整可操纵特征的信用评分模拟器。作者比较两阶段方法、无导数优化，以及不同更新频率的随机梯度方法，并按取得的样本量观察风险下降。两阶段方法在这些设置中更有效地优化了部署风险。实验还包括理论保证区间之外的较强反馈。  回音室为何可以被打破 这里的“回音室”是：模型持续使用自己造成的数据，却没有把自己造成数据变化的方式纳入优化。估计环境响应，让优化能够考虑另一种部署会带来什么结果。 Berk–Nash检查哪些信念能够支持行动；本研究检查优化是否计算了行动对未来数据的影响。对 RSI，后一个问题把修改的评价延伸到它造成的后续反馈。  原文与阅读位置 Miller、Perdomo 与 Zrnic，2021， Outside the Echo Chamber: Optimizing the Performative Risk ，ICML。论文页面及正式 PDF。 稳定与最优的差别见 §1.1；理论、算法和实验见 §3–5、Figures 1–2。", "paper": "Miller, Perdomo & Zrnic (2021). Outside the Echo Chamber: Optimizing the Performative Risk. ICML."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "model-collapse", "title": "模型崩溃 为什么只学习上一代输出会逐渐丢失内容", "category": "反馈与学习动力学", "text": "模型崩溃 为什么只学习上一代输出会逐渐丢失内容 如果一代模型只学习上一代生成的样本，少见的内容能保存多久？Shumailov 等研究的过程是：训练模型、从模型采样、再用这些样本训练下一代。每次采样和拟合的误差，也会进入下一代的数据。  一次遗漏怎样改变后续数据 一个低概率事件可能没有出现在某代有限的生成样本中。下一代只看到这批样本，可能进一步降低该事件的概率，随后更难生成它。一次采样中的遗漏，因而改变了后来的采样分布。 模型拟合还会增加误差。有限数据和给定模型类只能近似目标分布；这个近似又成为新数据源。下一代既继承上游偏差，也加入自己的采样与拟合误差。  固定样本量下的代际替换 论文的离散和高斯分析考察每代只使用上一模型生成的数据、样本量保持固定的情形。离散分析展示有限采样如何使某些类别消失。 在所分析的高斯过程中，协方差几乎必然趋于零；相对原始分布的期望平方 Wasserstein-2 距离却发散。平方距离衡量把两个分布匹配起来所需的最小平均平方移动距离。因此，典型轨迹上的分布越来越窄，并不意味着它在期望距离上越来越接近原分布。  语言模型中的数据来源对照 作者让语言模型反复在生成文本上微调，与持续混入原始数据的设置比较，观察少见内容和分布细节怎样变化；还改变生成时的重复惩罚，检验采样方式的影响。 实验支持保留原始数据对缓解退化的作用。两组主设置同时改变了每代遍历训练数据的次数，即 epochs，这项比较同时涉及数据来源与训练量。  与 GAN、Berk–Nash 一起看反馈 从反馈过程比较，模型崩溃研究谁产生下一轮数据；Berk–Nash研究行动选择哪些世界回复、主观模型怎样解释它们；有限判别器的 GAN 理论研究样本已经拿到后，评价器能识别哪些差异。 数据累积通过保留原始与历代数据改变误差传递路径。主题解读反馈怎样纠正学习者将数据来源、证据选择和分辨能力连起来，并以棋类与代码说明有效反馈怎样支持改进。  原文与阅读位置 Shumailov 等，2024， AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data ，Nature 631，755–759。正式全文。 过程定义见 Definition 2.1；理论分析见 Theoretical intuition、Theorem 3.1；语言模型实验及重复惩罚比较见实验部分。纯合成数据的条件采用2025年作者勘误修正后的版本。", "paper": "Shumailov et al. (2024). AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data. Nature 631:755–759; 含2025修订."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "data-accumulation", "title": "数据累积 为什么保留历史会改变自训练的长期误差", "category": "反馈与学习动力学", "text": "数据累积 为什么保留历史会改变自训练的长期误差 每代用新生成的数据替换旧数据，还是保留历代数据？Gerstgrasser 等改变这一训练协议，研究 模型崩溃中的误差怎样传递。  保留历史怎样约束后续误差 替换协议让下一代只依赖上一代生成的新数据。某代的偏差进入整批新数据，更早的信息不再直接参与拟合。累积协议保留原始数据，并加入各代新样本，使后续模型持续受到历史数据的约束。 论文在线性回归中精确比较两种协议。原始数据含 T 个样本，每个输入有 d 个维度；各代复用这些输入组成的设计矩阵，只重新生成输出。生成时加入独立、方差为 σ² 的高斯噪声。下面的公式采用等方差高斯输入，要求设计矩阵满列秩，且 T ≥ d+2。 令 C＝σ²d/(T−d−1)，第 n 代模型估计造成的期望额外测试均方误差为：   替换时，每代加入同量级的误差，误差随代数增长。累积时，后期误差项的权重逐渐减小，求和有有限上界。保留历史使误差有界，而非归零。  深度模型中的对照 作者在语言、分子和图像生成任务中比较替换与累积。语言模型每代重新初始化训练；另外设置随代数扩大数据量的替换对照，检验改善是否只是因为样本增多。 累积在若干任务中缓解了退化，图像变分自编码器仍可能流失细节。累积协议使用更多样本和梯度步骤。语言实验的原始参考语料是由模型生成的 TinyStories，这里的“原始”指循环开始时保留的数据。  记忆在反馈循环中的作用 数据累积让已有信息不容易被后续误差覆盖。在 RSI 中，它回答过去学到的东西如何保存；新反馈、表示扩展和更新规则则决定以后还能学到什么。  原文与阅读位置 Gerstgrasser 等，2024， Is Model Collapse Inevitable? Breaking the Curse of Recursion by Accumulating Real and Synthetic Data 。arXiv v2。 数据协议与实验见 §2.1–2.3；线性回归分析见 §3.1–3.2、Theorems 1–2；对照设置见 Appendix C。", "paper": "Gerstgrasser et al. (2024). Is Model Collapse Inevitable? Breaking the Curse of Recursion by Accumulating Real and Synthetic Data. arXiv v2."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "reward-overoptimization", "title": "奖励过优化 为什么代理分数提高而参考表现下降", "category": "反馈与学习动力学", "text": "奖励过优化 为什么代理分数提高而参考表现下降 一个评分器可以帮助系统找到较好的回答，也可能在被反复优化后把系统引向错误方向。Gao、Schulman 与 Hilton 研究：当生成策略越来越努力地提高代理评分，另一个固定参考模型会怎样评价它？  优化器会偏爱被高估的输出 代理评分只是对目标评价的近似。最初，提高代理分数可能也提高参考分数。但搜索越深入，系统越容易找到被代理高估的输出。当高估使一个回答的分数超过其他候选时，它就更容易被选中；被持续高估的一类回答则可能成为策略偏好的方向。 随机回答上的评分误差较小，仍可能在选取最高分回答时产生较大偏差。优化改变了输出分布，也改变了系统实际遇到哪些评价误差。  用两个评分器观察分歧怎样扩大 实验先固定一个参考奖励模型，用它给回答打分，再用这些标签训练较小的代理奖励模型。优化阶段只把代理分数交给生成系统；参考模型保持冻结，用来检查优化后的回答。 作者比较两种优化方式：生成多个候选回答，选出代理评分最高者；或用强化学习更新策略，让高代理分数的输出更容易出现。随后改变奖励模型规模和训练数据量，观察优化加强时两种评分的变化。 实验中，代理分数可以一直提高，而参考分数先提高、随后下降。更好的代理模型通常能够延缓或减轻这种偏离。代理能在一段范围内指导改进，持续优化则逐渐暴露其误差。  评价误差怎样被优化放大 有限判别器理论讨论评价器无法识别哪些差异。奖励过优化进一步追问：当优化器专门追求这个评价器的高分时，遗漏与误差怎样改变输出？ Berk–Nash让行动选择的证据反过来影响信念；奖励过优化在评价器完全冻结时也会发生。对 RSI，应检查系统修改后是否更集中地产生被当前评分器高估的行为，并用固定参考标准评价这些输出。  原文与阅读位置 Gao、Schulman 与 Hilton，2023， Scaling Laws for Reward Model Overoptimization ，ICML。论文页面及正式 PDF。 模型与数据设置见 §2.1；两种优化方式、经验曲线及模型规模比较见 §3.1–3.6；进一步讨论见 §4.5。", "paper": "Gao, Schulman & Hilton (2023). Scaling Laws for Reward Model Overoptimization. ICML."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "limited-discriminators", "title": "有限判别器 分不清两个分布不等于它们相同", "category": "反馈与学习动力学", "text": "有限判别器 分不清两个分布不等于它们相同 生成对抗网络让生成器产生样本，再由判别器判断样本来自真实数据还是生成器。Arora 等研究：判别器分不清两者时，生成器学到了真实分布，还是只满足了判别器能够检查的要求？  判别能力决定了怎样衡量接近 P 是真实分布，Q 是生成分布，𝓕 是允许使用的判别函数集合。每个函数 f 给样本一个评分。下面的距离取所有允许函数能发现的最大平均评分差异：   期望分别对真实样本和生成样本计算。距离小，表示这组判别器难以区分两个分布；判别器不会检查的结构，其差异也可能被遗漏。  泛化保证与多样性遗漏可以同时成立 训练样本上分不清，可能只是样本太少。论文在测量函数有界、网络参数满足平滑性等条件下，证明有限样本能够估计相应的神经网络距离。因此，受限判别器眼中的接近可以从样本推广到总体分布。 但作者还构造了支持点很少的分布：它让有限判别器难以区分，却缺少真实分布中的大量多样性。增加数据可以更准确地估计受限距离，判别器能检查的范围却仍由其函数集合决定。 论文还用生成器与判别器的混合方法进行图像实验，比较样本质量和生成指标。理论构造说明有限判别能力为何允许遗漏，实验检验混合训练方法的效果。  与 Berk–Nash、模型崩溃一起看反馈 从反馈过程比较，有限判别器理论研究收到样本后能识别什么；Berk–Nash研究行动怎样选择证据，再由主观模型解释证据；模型崩溃研究上一代模型成为数据源后，遗漏和误差怎样传递。 奖励过优化进一步考察追求当前评分怎样改变输出。对 RSI，评价器的分辨能力影响哪些改进能够被识别与保留。主题解读反馈怎样纠正学习者连起这些环节，并以棋类与代码说明有效反馈的作用。  原文与阅读位置 Arora 等，2017， Generalization and Equilibrium in Generative Adversarial Nets (GANs) ，ICML。论文页面及正式 PDF。 泛化结果见 §3.3、Theorem 3.1；有限支持构造见 §3.4、Corollary 3.1；图像实验见 §6。上式以线性测量形式解释受限距离，原论文允许更一般的测量函数。", "paper": "Arora et al. (2017). Generalization and Equilibrium in Generative Adversarial Nets (GANs). ICML."}, {"lang": "zh", "slug": "feedback", "title": "反馈怎样纠正学习者", "category": "反馈与学习动力学", "text": "反馈怎样纠正学习者 优秀反馈能把一次尝试变成下一次改进的依据。Berk–Nash、GAN 和模型崩溃从不同位置解释这条循环： 行动决定看到哪些证据，评价器决定读出哪些差异，数据来源决定哪些信息进入下一代。  棋类和代码则展示了明确、可执行的反馈怎样帮助学习。  Berk–Nash 行动决定检验哪些判断 在Berk–Nash均衡中，学习者的信念只支持最符合当前行动数据的模型，实际行动又在这个信念下最优。行动选择证据，证据支持解释，解释再支持行动。 因此，即使回复来自真实世界，未尝试行动上的错误仍可能留下；如果主观模型类错设，已见反馈也可能无法被正确解释。探索扩大检验范围，模型修正扩大可考虑的解释。两者共同决定真实反馈能够纠正什么。  GAN 评价器决定哪些差异能用于学习 GAN 的判别器同时看到真实样本和生成样本，学习区分来源，再把差异转成生成器的训练信号。真实样本持续提供参照，判别器负责把参照变成可用反馈。 Arora等的有限判别器结果表明，缺少大量多样性的生成分布仍可能让受限判别器难以区分。与Berk–Nash对证据选择的分析相连，这里要检查的是：证据已经拿到，评价器能否识别其中重要的差别？增加样本与增强判别能力分别改善这两个环节。  模型崩溃 数据来源决定怎样继承误差 模型崩溃研究上一代模型生成数据、下一代再从这些数据学习的过程。有限采样漏掉少见内容，可能让后代进一步降低它的概率；拟合误差也随新的数据源传递。 与持续接收世界回复或真实样本相比，这种代际替换会改变纠错依据本身。数据累积研究通过保留原始与历代数据改变误差传递路径。把三条文献线放在一起，便能分别追踪反馈的覆盖、分辨能力与来源。  棋类 规则检查与搜索共同提供训练依据 棋类是优秀反馈的例子：规则明确，局面转移可计算，终局结果可核验。AlphaZero用当前网络估计走法和局面价值，再以树搜索选择行动。对局结束后，同一网络用规则结算的结果学习局面价值，用搜索产生的走法分布学习策略；更新后的网络继续参与自博弈。 系统自己产生对局，但胜负由规则判定。搜索把多步模拟与网络估计结合起来，训练又把部分搜索经验变成以后更便宜的判断。自博弈因此能反复产生经验，让可靠的终局反馈与更充分的计算共同帮助学习。 已知规则不等于已经算出全部后果。棋类的进步可以来自不断算出、积累和复用这些后果，这也说明计算能够提高已有知识的可用程度。  代码 执行把候选变成可检验的结果 代码是另一个优秀反馈的例子：候选程序可以交给编译器和测试运行，得到编译错误、运行错误、测试失败或通过等明确结果。CodeRL据此训练评价网络，再把执行结果加工成生成模型的训练信号。 CodeRL在生成阶段还利用题面示例测试筛选候选，用评价网络选择前缀并重新生成。它分别比较训练信号和再生成步骤：训练对照支持用评价网络为代码中的词元分配训练权重，后一组显示候选集合更容易包含通过测试的程序。这里可以清楚区分两种收益：学习器吸收执行反馈，以及生成时利用检查来修订候选。 编译和测试为给定要求提供明确、可重复的检查。模型负责提出程序，执行器给出结果，学习再把成功与失败转成后续生成的依据。这比只让模型给自己的代码打分，多了一条可执行的纠错通路。  反馈怎样接上递归改进 棋类和代码把反馈的积极作用展示得很清楚：结果有明确依据，尝试能够反复进行，经验可以用于改进下一次选择。Berk–Nash、GAN与模型崩溃则帮助辨认这条通路中的不同环节。 RSI进一步问，系统能否改进获取、解释和利用反馈的方法。可塑性研究关注持续吸收经验的能力，STOP与HyperAgents把负责产生修改的程序也纳入学习。反馈由此不仅评价当前行为，也成为改进后续学习机制的依据。  原文与阅读位置 - Berk–Nash、有限 GAN 判别器、模型崩溃与数据累积的原文、版本和条件，见上面各篇独立解读的文末。 - Silver 等，2018， A general reinforcement learning algorithm that masters chess, shogi, and Go through self-play ，Science。正式出处；作者全文及补充材料。正文第2—3页及式（1）说明训练循环；补充材料 Domain Knowledge、Search、Configuration 说明规则和探索；图1—3报告对局评测。 - Le 等，2022， CodeRL: Mastering Code Generation through Pretrained Models and Deep Reinforcement Learning ，NeurIPS。会议正文；补充材料。§3.3与图2说明执行反馈和训练，§4.1说明训练顺序；图3和补充算法1说明筛选与再生成，表2、表4分别比较训练信号与生成步骤。", "paper": ""}];
